content-briefs/gp-referral.md:35:GP referral, general practitioner, specialist referral, Medicare rebate, Medicare item number, bulk billed, referral validity, 12 months, bariatric surgeon, FRACS, St John of God, BMI, comorbidities, type 2 diabetes, sleep apnoea, private health cover, consultation, pre-operative assessment, self-referral, medical history, dietitian, accredited hospital
content-briefs/gp-referral.md:64:Define a GP referral plainly: a letter from the patient's GP asking a bariatric surgeon at Obesity Surgery WA to assess and treat them. Frame it as the standard, expected way the Australian system connects patients to specialist care, and as the normal first step for weight loss surgery.
content-briefs/gp-referral.md:73:Practical, dual-purpose guidance: what a good referral should contain (current weight/height or BMI, relevant medical history, weight-related conditions, current medications, previous weight loss attempts), the 12-month validity of a standard GP referral, and the key distinction — you can self-refer and be seen, but only a GP referral unlocks Medicare rebates. This serves both patients and referring GPs.
content-briefs/gp-referral.md:77:- For patients: ask your GP for a referral to Obesity Surgery WA; the practice arranges the consultation
content-briefs/gp-referral.md:79:- For GPs: what a referral should note (BMI, medical and weight history, comorbidities, medications, previous attempts)
content-briefs/gp-referral.md:82:- Include the BMI starting-point note (35+, or 30+ with related conditions) as a starting point only, confirmed clinically at consultation
content-briefs/gp-referral.md:90:5. **Hospital Recovery** — recovery in hospital, usually a few nights (NOT "3–4 nights"), with nursing and dietitian support
content-briefs/gp-referral.md:115:Explain costs depend on the procedure, private health cover level, and individual circumstances. State that a valid GP referral is what enables Medicare rebates, and that most bariatric procedures attract a Medicare item number. Note that patients with appropriate private hospital cover typically pay a gap rather than the full fee, and that early superannuation access is an option some use. Emphasise a clear written estimate is provided before any commitment. Do NOT quote dollar figures.
content-briefs/gp-referral.md:136:2. How do I get a referral for weight loss surgery? (Book a GP appointment, ask for a referral to Obesity Surgery WA; can call (08) 9332 0066 for help knowing what to ask)
content-briefs/gp-referral.md:139:5. What should my GP include in the referral? (Weight/height or BMI, medical history, comorbidities, medications, previous attempts; brief referral is enough to start)
content-briefs/gp-referral.md:140:6. I am a GP. How do I refer a patient to Obesity Surgery WA? (Address referral to the practice; patient books, or phone (08) 9332 0066 to arrange — no invented fax/email)
content-briefs/gp-referral.md:142:8. Where will my consultation be, and which hospitals are used? (Booragoon and Mandurah rooms; surgery at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco)
