content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:8:This is a high-intent landing page for people who already know the term "bariatric surgery" and are comparing providers in Perth. It needs to establish Obesity Surgery WA's credentials and depth of team, explain the procedure range clearly, and move the reader toward a consultation.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:33:sleeve gastrectomy, Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, one anastomosis (mini) gastric bypass, adjustable gastric band, laparoscopic keyhole surgery, excess weight loss, BMI eligibility, type 2 diabetes management, ghrelin / hunger hormones, Medicare and private health cover, revision bariatric surgery, medical weight management, dietitian-led aftercare, St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco, FRACS bariatric surgeon. Any GLP-1 or active-ingredient reference is subject to the pending TGA/legal decision: use active-ingredient/class names only and never brand names.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:36:SERP is almost entirely commercial Perth bariatric clinic/surgeon homepages and service landing pages — Perth Surgical & Bariatrics (#1/#6), Obesity Surgery WA (#2, the target site, already ranking), Aurora Bariatrics (#3, named-surgeon angle), Perth Weight Loss Surgery / Dr Siva Gounder (#5), Mercy Bariatrics (#7, highest authority at DR 31), Advance Surgical, Evolve, Veritas, WA Weight Loss. Domain authority is low across the board (DR 1–31), so this is a content-quality and relevance battle, not a backlink arms race. Google also surfaces a People Also Ask block (cost, Medicare coverage, disqualifiers, the "30/30 rule") signalling strong informational sub-intent layered under the commercial query. Winning pages combine provider credentials + clear procedure menu + cost/Medicare answers.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:48:Competitors lead with either a single named surgeon or a generic procedure list; the differentiation gap is a team-based, multi-disciplinary positioning (a dedicated APD dietitian team, 20+ yrs, two SJOG hospitals) which the brief already leans into — own this. Hospital names and affiliations must be verified with the client before publish. Biggest on-page SERP gap: the PAA block shows Google wants direct, plain-language answers to (1) bariatric surgery cost in Perth, (2) whether it's covered by Medicare/private health in Australia, (3) what disqualifies a candidate / BMI eligibility, and (4) the gastric sleeve vs bypass difference — most competitor homepages bury or omit these. The FAQ section in the brief already maps to these; ensure those FAQs are marked up with FAQ schema to capture PAA/rich-result real estate. Secondary gap: no competitor clearly integrates medical weight management alongside surgical options — covering the full pathway (medical + surgical + revision) addresses readers not yet ready for surgery and widens topical coverage. Any GLP-1 or active-ingredient wording remains subject to the pending TGA/legal decision; use only active-ingredient/class names and no brands. Note AHPRA/TGA limits mean the patient-story/testimonial angle is off-limits, so the win must come from honesty (frank risks section), credentialism, and answering buyer questions better than rivals.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:57:- Subheadline: 2–3 sentences. Acknowledge the reader's experience of having already tried other things. Name the procedures offered (gastric sleeve, gastric bypass, mini bypass, gastric band, revision surgery, medical weight management). Mention both hospital locations (SJOG Murdoch and Subiaco). Keep it grounding and specific.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:73:- Para 3: Explain that most procedures are performed laparoscopically (keyhole), using small incisions. Do not make comparative pain, recovery or safety claims; explain that the surgeon will discuss the technique, expected recovery, alternatives and individual risks. Make clear that procedure choice is never one-size-fits-all — the surgeon advises based on weight, health conditions, eating patterns, and the patient's goals.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:77:- BMI summary to anchor the section: "BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold; discuss any other clinical pathway with your surgeon."
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:79:  - BMI 40+: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements; BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:80:  - BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:81:  - BMI below 35: this does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; discuss any non-MBS clinical options with the surgeon, who will assess suitability individually
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:84:  - Final eligibility: always determined after a full assessment with the surgeon and wider team, including medical history review and review of conditions that affect surgical risk
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:92:4. **Pre-surgery preparation** — may include a pre-operative diet and practical guidance on the planned procedure and early recovery
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:93:5. **Your procedure at SJOG Murdoch or Subiaco** — laparoscopic in most cases; short hospital stay; surgeon explains the individual recovery plan
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:107:- Intro paragraph: Acknowledge every operation carries risk. Explain that laparoscopic techniques use small incisions, without making comparative safety, pain or recovery claims. Commit to giving the reader a frank picture rather than something they find out later.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:109:  - General surgical risks: bleeding, infection, blood clots, anaesthetic reactions
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:117:Cover: total cost depends on procedure, private health cover, level of excess (out of pocket), hospital and anaesthetic fees; Medicare and private-health benefits may apply when the relevant criteria are met, but coverage and gaps vary. Any range or estimate should be discussed at consultation with an itemised breakdown. Superannuation access may be available only under specific circumstances — verify current rules. Do not quote specific dollar figures.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:126:Include concise, answer-first responses to these questions: how much bariatric surgery costs in Perth (no dollar figures; any range is discussed at consultation); Medicare and private-health coverage; how to choose a procedure; sleeve versus bypass; surgical and non-surgical options; the 30/30 eating rule; who may not meet MBS or clinical suitability requirements; aftercare and what can vary over 10 years; where appointments and surgery take place (hospital identity to be verified); whether revision is possible; and how to assess a bariatric surgeon in Perth. Any GLP-1 or active-ingredient wording remains subject to the pending TGA/legal decision and must never use brand names.
content-briefs/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:134:- Reader: a Perth adult who already knows what bariatric surgery is; they are comparing providers and want to trust this team with something significant; credentialism (FRACS, SJOG hospitals, 20+ years) matters here
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:33:bariatric surgery, gastric sleeve, sleeve gastrectomy, gastric bypass, adjustable gastric band, lap band, obesity as a medical condition, BMI, GLP-1 medication, very low calorie diet, dietitian support, type 2 diabetes, Medicare rebate, private health cover, endoscopic option, weight regain, Booragoon, Mandurah
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:36:The SERP is almost entirely commercial: individual Perth bariatric surgeon and clinic homepages (Dr Siva Gounder / perthweightlosssurgery.com.au, perthbariatricsurgery.com.au / Dr Andrew Kiyingi, Advance Surgical / Jon Armstrong, Aurora Bariatrics / Dr Ruth Blackham, Dr Kevin Dolan, Perth Surgical & Bariatrics, Evolve Bariatrics). Domain ratings are low across the board (DR 1-26), so this is a winnable, weakly-defended SERP. Almost every result is a surgeon-led homepage selling a procedure menu — not informational guides. People Also Ask boxes surface clear informational intent: "How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?", "Is weight loss surgery covered by Medicare in Australia?", and "What BMI is used to assess weight loss surgery?". Intent is mixed commercial + informational, which fits the brief's positioning of this as the broad top-of-funnel page.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:45:| 5 | obesitysurgerywa.com.au | Weight Loss Surgery Perth — Obesity Surgery WA (OSWA, DR 11) |
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:48:Every top result is a surgeon homepage that funnels straight to surgery — none present medical management (GLP-1 medication, VLCD, dietitian support) as an equal pathway. That is OSWA's biggest differentiation opportunity and aligns exactly with the brief's mandate to frame obesity as a medical condition and not over-index on surgery. Second gap: the PAA boxes show high demand for cost, Medicare coverage, and BMI/eligibility answers, yet competitor homepages bury or omit these — directly addressing how medical management is assessed from BMI 27+, how Medicare rebates and MBS thresholds work, and how much treatment may cost / whether superannuation access can be explored in plain language (the brief already mandates an eligibility section, cost paragraph, and FAQs) will capture featured-snippet and PAA real estate competitors leave open. Third gap: low-DR competitors mean strong topical depth, empathetic non-judgemental framing, and a team-based (dedicated APD dietitian team, physicians + surgeons, 2 clinics) angle can outrank them without heavy backlinks. Note OSWA (obesitysurgerywa.com.au) currently sits at position 5 with DR 11, so this page is an existing-asset improvement play, not a cold start.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:60:Use these factual anchors. Keep the BMI stat explicitly assessment-only; it is not a surgical MBS threshold:
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:67:| Medical-team assessment from | BMI 27+ | Individual assessment; not a surgical MBS threshold |
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:79:- BMI summary to anchor the section: "You do not need a very high BMI to start a conversation. A BMI of 27 or above, particularly where a weight-related health condition is present, is a reason to request an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options. For the Medicare-listed surgical pathway, current MBS thresholds refer to BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity. We will help you understand what needs to be assessed."
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:81:  - BMI around 27+ with a condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea: invite an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:82:  - Current MBS surgical thresholds: BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity; clinical suitability is assessed individually
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:83:  - Higher BMI with prior attempts at weight loss that did not hold: surgical and non-surgical options can be assessed together
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:109:- Intro paragraph: Acknowledge that every option on the spectrum carries its own considerations. Frame this as a genuine service to the reader — the team will not pretend otherwise. Medications have side effects. Very low calorie diets need supervision. Surgery is a significant step with real risks and recovery. The clinician should explain how these considerations relate to the reader's medical situation before any commitment.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:136:9. What is the 30/30 eating rule after surgery?
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:146:- MBS surgical threshold: state BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity as assessment guidance; BMI below 35 does not meet that MBS threshold. Do not present a lower-BMI statement as surgical eligibility.
