content-briefs/hiatus-hernia-repair.md:35:The AU SERP for hiatus hernia terms mixes large informational/authority pages (Healthdirect, Better Health Channel, Mayo Clinic, Cleveland Clinic) on the head and definitional terms with surgeon- and clinic-owned procedure pages (upper GI and general surgery practices) on the commercial "surgery / repair / cost" terms. Cost-modified queries skew commercial: searchers want a number and want to know what drives it. Pages are typically structured around what a hiatus hernia is, symptoms, when surgery is needed, the laparoscopic/fundoplication procedure, recovery and risks, and a cost/funding section.
content-briefs/hiatus-hernia-repair.md:38:(1) Cost transparency — most AU pages either omit cost or give a vague range; an explicit explanation of Medicare contribution, private health fund cover and the itemised-written-estimate approach fills the exact intent behind "hiatus hernia surgery cost". (2) Bariatric crossover — almost no competitor connects hiatus hernia / reflux to bariatric surgery (sleeve-worsened reflux, hernia repaired alongside weight-loss surgery); this practice's upper GI specialism is a genuine differentiator. (3) Honest "do you even need surgery" framing — many commercial pages push toward the operation; a page that openly says medication may be the better path for some readers earns trust. (4) Recovery detail — "hiatus hernia surgery recovery" has clean volume and is under-served with practical, plain-language detail (diet stages, swallowing, return to activity). Compliance note: AHPRA means no testimonials, before/after, or guaranteed-outcome language — competitors that overclaim leave room for a trust-led, compliant page.
