content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:57:2. **Two-system mental model.** Most competitors explain Medicare OR cost in isolation; few cleanly model the two-system interaction (Medicare for doctors, private cover for hospital, patient pays the gap) in one mental model — the brief's core angle.
content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:81:| Hospital and theatre | Not Medicare | Funded through your private hospital cover, not Medicare |
content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:90:1. **Medicare's role:** Explain that Medicare pays a set rebate toward eligible medical services. Name the specific item numbers that apply: 31575 (sleeve gastrectomy), 31572 (Roux-en-Y bypass), and others in the MBS bariatric surgery family (31569–31581). Also covers the anaesthetist, physician and dietitian consultations linked to surgery. Emphasise clearly: Medicare does NOT cover the hospital stay, theatre fees or accommodation.
content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:91:2. **Private health's role:** Explain that hospital costs are where private health insurance comes in. Generally a gold tier policy — or equivalent that specifically includes bariatric surgery and lists the relevant item numbers including 31575 and 31572 — is needed. Flag the key trap: not every gold policy includes bariatric surgery; confirming the specific item numbers is essential.
content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:92:3. **The interaction and the gap:** Describe how the two systems work side by side — Medicare for the doctors, private cover for the hospital, and the remaining out-of-pocket portion (gap, excess, copayment) for the patient. Acknowledge that the 12-month waiting period, pre-approval requirements and excess amounts mean each person's real position looks different. The financial coordinator is the practical next step.
content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:97:- **BMI note:** Cover is generally relevant for patients with a BMI of 35 or above, or 30 or above with related health conditions, who are clinically suitable for surgery. The patient's fund may have its own criteria on top of the clinical ones.
content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:100:  2. Medicare does not cover hospital theatre fees or accommodation — that is the role of private hospital cover.
content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:101:  3. Private hospital cover generally needs to be gold tier or equivalent and must specifically include bariatric surgery item numbers.
content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:126:4. SJOG's agreed rates with most major funds can meaningfully reduce out-of-pocket costs compared to a hospital without such arrangements.
content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:138:  5. Medicare rebates are fixed schedule amounts and do not cover hospital or theatre — without suitable private cover, hospital costs become a major liability.
content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:146:- The hospital side and how SJOG's agreed rates with major funds reduce out-of-pocket costs for many patients.
content-briefs/medicare-private-health-cover.md:154:- **Realistic ways to fund the gap:** Explain the practical sources patients use to cover the out-of-pocket portion: private health cover (hospital and theatre), Medicare rebates (the eligible medical items), early release of superannuation on compassionate/medical grounds (cross-reference the superannuation page for detail), and that SOME patients choose to use third-party / external medical finance providers. Frame these as options the patient can consider, not a recommended path.
content-briefs/gastric-band.md:35:adjustable gastric band, laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding, lap band, silicone band, gastric pouch, access port, saline adjustment, band slippage, band erosion, keyhole surgery, reversible weight loss surgery, excess weight loss, BMI, Medicare item 31569, bariatric surgeon Perth, St John of God Murdoch, gastric sleeve, gastric bypass
content-briefs/gastric-band.md:38:The SERP is almost entirely commercial clinic and surgeon procedure pages (Advance Surgical #1, Obesity Surgery WA #2, Dr Kevin Dolan #3, Perth Weight Loss Surgery #4, Evolve Bariatrics #5, Aurora Bariatrics, Veritas, WA Weight Loss, Perth Surgical & Bariatrics). No informational hospital portals, no editorial comparison articles, no government health pages rank on page 1 — this is a transactional, local-intent SERP. Domain Ratings are low (DR 1–26), so the keyword is highly winnable on content quality and topical depth rather than authority. Google surfaces a People Also Ask cluster heavily weighted to cost and Medicare ("How much does bariatric surgery cost in Perth?", "How much does a gastric band cost in Australia?", "Is gastric band surgery covered by Medicare?") plus a procedure-vs-drug comparison ("Mounjaro or gastric sleeve?").
