docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:79:> Obesity Surgery WA has supported Perth patients for more than 20 years, with a specialist bariatric team offering gastric sleeve, gastric bypass, mini bypass, gastric band and revision surgery, plus medical weight management, dietitian support and exercise physiology at current OSWA clinic and hospital locations.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:81:Why it matters: The hospital names conflict with the brief, but the draft may reflect newer client information, so verify the correct hospital name(s) with the client before changing either direction.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:87:> 20+ yrs bariatric experience | Current hospital locations to be verified | APD dietitian-led aftercare | 4+ surgical pathways including sleeve, bypass, mini bypass, band and revision.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:95:> 1. Initial enquiry and GP referral: most patients start with a GP referral, which can help with Medicare and private health requirements; if you contact OSWA directly, the team can guide you on what is needed. 2. Consultation with your surgeon: your surgeon reviews your history, goals and health conditions, then explains which surgical or non-surgical pathways may be appropriate. 3. Wider team assessment: this may include physician, dietitian and exercise physiology input. 4. Pre-surgery preparation: you receive procedure-specific preparation advice, including any pre-operative diet. 5. Procedure day: surgery is usually laparoscopic, with hospital stay and recovery guidance tailored to the procedure. 6. Long-term aftercare: follow-up, nutrition monitoring and habit support continue after surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:101:> Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y): restricts food intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70-80% of excess weight, though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2 diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin and mineral monitoring and supplementation.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:109:> Obesity Surgery WA has been a trusted name in Perth weight loss for over 20 years and offers gastric sleeve surgery, gastric bypass, mini bypass, gastric band, and revision surgery at Mount Private Hospital, The Park Private Hospital, and Waikiki Private Hospital.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:111:> Obesity Surgery WA offers [gastric sleeve surgery](/gastric-sleeve-perth), [gastric bypass surgery](/gastric-bypass-perth), [mini gastric bypass](/mini-gastric-bypass), [gastric band surgery](/gastric-band), [revision bariatric surgery](/revision-bariatric-surgery) and [non-surgical weight management](/non-surgical-weight-loss). See [eligibility](/am-i-eligible), [Medicare and private health cover](/medicare-private-health-cover), [GP referral](/gp-referral), [surgeons](/surgeons), [FAQs](/faqs) and [book a consultation](/book-consultation).
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:127:> FAQs: 1. How much does bariatric surgery cost in Perth? 2. Is bariatric surgery covered by Medicare or private health insurance? 3. How do I know which procedure is right for me? 4. What is the difference between gastric sleeve and gastric bypass? 5. Do I have to have surgery, or are there non-surgical options? 6. What is the 30/30 rule after bariatric surgery? 7. Who may not be eligible for bariatric surgery? 8. How should I choose a bariatric surgeon in Perth? Use the answer to question 8 to discuss credentials, FRACS/specialist registration, hospital access, aftercare and comfort with the team - do not claim OSWA is the best.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:129:Why it matters: The current search set includes cost in Perth, best bariatric surgeon in Perth, the 30/30 eating rule, who is not eligible, sleeve-vs-bypass or medication comparisons and long-term outcomes.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:169:> Obesity Surgery WA has been a trusted name in Perth weight loss for over 20 years and offers gastric sleeve surgery, gastric bypass, mini bypass, gastric band, and revision surgery at Mount Private Hospital, The Park Private Hospital, and Waikiki Private Hospital.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:171:> Reviewed by the OSWA bariatric surgical team. Last updated: [month year]. Surgeon credentials, specialist registration/FRACS status, hospital affiliations and APD dietitian support to be confirmed before publish.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:193:1. **Laparoscopic recovery comparison (source lines 74-79)**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:195:> Most bariatric surgeries in Australia are performed as laparoscopic keyhole surgeries, meaning less pain post-operatively, smaller scars, and generally, a faster recovery than open surgery. No procedure is a ‘one-size-fits-all’; your surgeon will spend time understanding your weight loss goals and recommend a procedure based on those, any accompanying health conditions, and your lifestyle.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:197:> Most bariatric surgeries are performed laparoscopically, using small incisions. Your surgeon will explain the technique, expected recovery, alternatives and individual risks, then discuss which procedure may be clinically appropriate for you.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:199:Why it matters: Remove the comparative pain and recovery claim; the remaining explanation is evidence-neutral and does not imply a better outcome.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:203:> Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y): restricts food intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70–80% of excess weight, though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2 diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin and mineral monitoring and supplementation.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:251:> Medicare rebates apply to most bariatric procedures when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance with a gold-tier or equivalent policy covers the hospital and theatre component. The combination of both typically means a significant portion of the total cost is covered. Out-of-pocket costs vary depending on your level of cover, excess, and anaesthetist fees. Our team provides a clear, itemised cost breakdown before any commitment is made, so you know exactly where you stand.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:275:### G2 — MBS-inaccurate BMI wording
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:279:> Bariatric surgery is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 40 or higher, or in specific circumstances for those with a BMI of 35 or higher when an obesity-related health condition is also present. People with a BMI between 30 and 35 may be candidates in specific circumstances, particularly where type 2 diabetes is poorly controlled.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:281:> BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold; discuss any other clinical pathway with your surgeon.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:283:2. **BMI 40+ bullet (source lines 101-102)**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:285:> BMI 40+: surgery is generally an appropriate option to discuss, regardless of other conditions
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:287:> BMI 40+: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements; BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:289:3. **BMI 35-40 bullet (source lines 104-107)**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:291:> BMI 35–40 and a related condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint disease, or gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD): surgery is commonly considered
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:293:> BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:295:4. **BMI 30-35 bullet (source lines 109-111)**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:297:> BMI 30–35: may be considered in select cases, often where diabetes is difficult to manage with medication alone; assessed on an individual basis
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:299:> BMI below 35: this does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; discuss any non-MBS clinical options with the surgeon, who will assess suitability individually.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:301:Why it matters: The MBS pathway is BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. Nothing below BMI 35 qualifies under MBS, and all BMI wording must remain assessment-only.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:314:  > **Pre-surgery preparation** Your surgeon will explain your procedure and individual recovery plan, and you may need to follow a specific pre-op diet to shrink your liver and reduce surgical risk (typically 2–4 weeks). But you won’t do it alone! Our staff will support you throughout.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:316:  > **Procedure day** Obesity Surgery WA’s bariatric surgery is performed via keyhole surgery in most cases, with short post-operative hospital stays (1-3 nights) at [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth. Guided by nursing and dietetic staff, you’ll start on fluids and your recovery will be monitored until you’re ready to return home.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:322:  > 1. **Initial enquiry and GP referral:** most patients start with a GP referral, which can help with Medicare and private health requirements; if you contact OSWA directly, the team can guide you on what is needed. 2. **Consultation with your surgeon:** your surgeon reviews your history, goals and health conditions, then explains which surgical or non-surgical pathways may be appropriate. 3. **Wider team assessment:** this may include physician, dietitian and exercise physiology input. 4. **Pre-surgery preparation:** you receive procedure-specific preparation advice, including any pre-operative diet. 5. **Procedure day:** surgery is usually laparoscopic, with hospital stay and recovery guidance tailored to the procedure. 6. **Long-term aftercare:** follow-up, nutrition monitoring and habit support continue after surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:328:  > **Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y)**: restricts food intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70–80% of excess weight, though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2 diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin and mineral monitoring and supplementation.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:341:  > - [**Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y)**](/gastric-bypass-perth): changes stomach size and how food passes through the small intestine. It has procedure-specific nutritional requirements and risks; your surgeon can explain why it may or may not be appropriate for your circumstances.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:351:  > General surgical risks include bleeding, infection, blood clots, and adverse reactions to anaesthetic — these apply to any surgical procedure.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:365:  > **General and procedure-specific surgical risks:** Bleeding, infection, blood clots, anaesthetic complications, leaks at staple or join lines, and band slippage or erosion can occur.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:377:  > **2. Is bariatric surgery available on Medicare or private health insurance?** Medicare rebates apply to most bariatric procedures when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance with a gold-tier or equivalent policy covers the hospital and theatre component. The combination of both typically means a significant portion of the total cost is covered. Out-of-pocket costs vary depending on your level of cover, excess, and anaesthetist fees. Our team provides a clear, itemised cost breakdown before any commitment is made, so you know exactly where you stand.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:383:  > **5. What does aftercare involve, and why does it matter so much?** Aftercare typically includes follow-up appointments with your surgeon, ongoing dietitian and exercise physiology support, nutritional monitoring via blood tests, and guidance on building sustainable habits. It matters because surgery changes your body, but long-term results are shaped by what happens in the months and years that follow. Patients who stay engaged with their care team consistently tend to achieve better long-term outcomes. At Obesity Surgery WA, we view your surgery as the beginning of your weight loss journey, not the end.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:385:  > **6. Where will my surgery and appointments take place?** Consultations take place at our clinics in Booragoon or Greenfields, with surgeries performed at [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth. Once your surgery is planned, our team will walk you through exactly what to expect, where to go, and who will be involved at every stage, so there are no surprises on the day.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:393:> Obesity Surgery WA offers [gastric sleeve surgery](/gastric-sleeve-perth), [gastric bypass surgery](/gastric-bypass-perth), [mini gastric bypass](/mini-gastric-bypass), [gastric band surgery](/gastric-band), [revision bariatric surgery](/revision-bariatric-surgery) and [non-surgical weight management](/non-surgical-weight-loss). See [eligibility](/am-i-eligible), [Medicare and private health cover](/medicare-private-health-cover), [GP referral](/gp-referral), [surgeons](/surgeons), [FAQs](/faqs) and [book a consultation](/book-consultation).
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:405:> There is no single Perth price. Total costs vary by procedure, hospital, surgeon, anaesthetist, tests, private-health cover, excess and waiting periods. Any range or estimate should be discussed at consultation with an itemised breakdown; ask your insurer which benefits and gaps apply before booking.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:423:> **6. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:425:> Many bariatric programs use a 30/30 approach: take about 30 minutes to eat and separate drinks from meals by about 30 minutes. It is a general postoperative guide, not a universal rule; your surgeon or dietitian will tailor advice to your procedure and stage of recovery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:429:> No online checklist can decide suitability. For MBS items, a BMI below 35 does not meet the threshold, and BMI 35–39.9 without at least one major obesity-related condition also does not meet the threshold; a procedure may also be deferred if assessment identifies unacceptable surgical or anaesthetic risk, unresolved medical issues or difficulty completing required follow-up. Your surgeon makes an individual assessment.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:437:> Appointments take place at the confirmed OSWA clinic locations, and surgery takes place at the hospital selected with your surgeon. Hospital names and affiliations must be verified before publication; the team should confirm the location, admission process and follow-up arrangements during planning.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:445:> Ask about specialist registration or FRACS status, relevant bariatric experience, verified hospital access, multidisciplinary aftercare and how risks, costs and complications are explained. Ask who provides follow-up and what happens if concerns arise. Avoid claims that any individual surgeon or clinic is “the best.”
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:471:- The risks instruction no longer says that keyhole techniques and experienced surgeons “make these procedures safe for most suitable candidates”; it now requires small-incision description without comparative safety, pain or recovery claims.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:472:- The BMI summary and bullets now state the MBS rule: BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment; BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:475:- The FAQ direction now includes Perth cost, Medicare coverage, the 30/30 rule, who may not meet MBS or clinical suitability requirements, 10-year variation and how to assess a surgeon.
