docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-sleeve-perth-codex.md:181:- **Blocking #2 (BMI 30–35 wording) — BRIEF-INHERITED.** The brief itself mandates this exact framing (`content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:88`: "BMI of 35 or above, or BMI 30–35 with weight-related health conditions"). The MBS-accuracy concern stands, but this is not writer error — the brief needs the same correction. Frame as "fix draft AND brief."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-sleeve-perth-codex.md:182:- **Blocking #3 (hospitals) — reframed: blocked until VERIFIED, do not rewrite either direction.** All five drafts consistently name Mount Private/The Park/Waikiki; this may be newer client info than the brief. Confirm with Marcus/client before changing anything; do not apply the SJOG rewrite suggested above until confirmed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-sleeve-perth-codex.md:185:- **ADDED Blocking (missed): absolute private-health coverage claims.** Draft: "private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the hospital and theatre component" and "will cover the hospital component." Gold-tier includes the category, but benefits are policy-, waiting-period- and gap-dependent — same cost-certainty class as the fee figures. Rewrite: "private health insurance with appropriate hospital cover (typically gold tier) may contribute to hospital and theatre costs; out-of-pockets vary by fund and policy."
src/app/osw/content/super-access-weight-loss-surgery/page.tsx:5:  title: "Using Super for Weight Loss Surgery Perth | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/super-access-weight-loss-surgery/page.tsx:36:    "There is no BMI threshold for accessing super itself. Eligibility is decided by the ATO based on your medical and financial circumstances, not your weight.",
src/app/osw/content/super-access-weight-loss-surgery/page.tsx:47:        "Start with a referral from your GP to Obesity Surgery WA. At your consultation we confirm whether surgery is clinically appropriate and which procedure suits you, so you have a clear treatment plan and an estimate of costs to attach to your application.",
src/app/osw/content/super-access-weight-loss-surgery/page.tsx:81:    "It can be combined with private health insurance or a payment plan to cover the full cost of your procedure and hospital stay.",
src/app/osw/content/super-access-weight-loss-surgery/page.tsx:99:        "Lead bariatric surgeon at Obesity Surgery WA, lecturer at the University of Notre Dame, and someone who has personally been through bariatric surgery, bringing both clinical expertise and lived understanding to every patient.",
src/app/osw/content/super-access-weight-loss-surgery/page.tsx:119:        "The ATO approves an amount that reasonably covers the cost of the treatment and related expenses, such as the surgeon's fee, hospital and anaesthetist costs, and travel where relevant. It is based on the quotes and estimates you provide, not a fixed figure, and it cannot exceed what your fund can release. As a guide, if your out-of-pocket gap after Medicare and fund rebates is $6,000, the ATO assesses applications for that specific documented amount.",
src/app/osw/content/super-access-weight-loss-surgery/page.tsx:144:        "Often, yes. Many patients use their private health cover to reduce the hospital and procedure costs, then access a smaller amount of super to cover the remaining gap. We can help you understand what your cover contributes so you only release what you actually need.",
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:5:1. Medication/BMI eligibility is too absolute and does not match the brief's lower-BMI medical-team entry point.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:7:Quote: "Medical weight management options including GLP-1s may be suitable if you have a BMI of 30 or higher."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:9:Suggested rewrite: "Non-surgical medical weight management may be worth discussing from a BMI of 27 or above, particularly where weight-related health conditions are present. Medication suitability depends on the specific medicine, your medical history and current Australian prescribing guidance, while surgical Medicare eligibility is assessed separately."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:35:Suggested rewrite: "If you are researching weight loss surgery Perth options, Obesity Surgery WA can help you compare surgical, medical and dietitian-led pathways without pressure."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:45:Quote: "Obesity Surgery WA is a specialist team of surgeons, physicians, dietitians, and exercise physiologists who have been supporting weight loss in Perth for over 20 years."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:47:Suggested rewrite: "Obesity Surgery WA brings together surgeons, physicians, Accredited Practising Dietitians and exercise physiologists, with 20+ years supporting weight loss in Perth, clinics in Booragoon and Mandurah, and medical-team conversations available from a starting BMI of 27+ where clinically appropriate."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:51:Quote: "You may be eligible for weight loss surgery if you have a BMI of 40 or higher, or 35 or higher with a weight-related health condition such as type 2 diabetes or high blood pressure, and where diet and exercise alone have not produced long-term results."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:53:Suggested rewrite: "A BMI of 27 or above, particularly with a weight-related health condition, may be enough to start a conversation with our medical team about non-surgical options. Surgical options are usually discussed at higher BMI ranges; current MBS guidance for clinically severe obesity generally refers to BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with major co-morbidities, with suitability assessed by your surgeon."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:59:Suggested rewrite: "Is weight loss surgery covered by Medicare in Australia? Medicare rebates may apply to eligible specialist consultations and some bariatric surgery items when current MBS criteria are met. Private health cover, waiting periods, hospital excess and anaesthetist fees still affect out-of-pocket costs, so our team gives you an itemised estimate before you make any decision."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:63:Quote: "The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:65:Suggested rewrite: "The cost of weight loss treatment in Perth depends on the pathway: medical management, dietitian-led programs and surgery all have different fee structures, and surgical costs also vary by procedure, private health cover, hospital excess and anaesthetist fees."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:106:Main fixes: separate surgical eligibility from medical management, add direct Medicare/minimum-BMI/cost FAQs, and cut 600+ words.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:117:- **Blocking #1 (GLP-1 "suitable if BMI 30+") stays blocking** — the specific numeric suitability claim is the draft's own. Note the GLP-1 topic itself is brief-mandated (the brief positions medical management as an equal pathway); the TGA class-reference question is a site-wide policy decision for Marcus/legal (see hub adjudication).
src/app/osw/content/mini-gastric-bypass/page.tsx:5:  title: "Mini Gastric Bypass Perth | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/mini-gastric-bypass/page.tsx:7:    "Mini gastric bypass in Perth with Obesity Surgery WA. A simpler single-loop bypass for lasting weight loss. Understand how it differs from a standard bypass.",
src/app/osw/content/mini-gastric-bypass/page.tsx:17:    "Mini gastric bypass in Perth with Obesity Surgery WA. A simpler single-loop bypass for lasting weight loss. Understand how it differs from a standard bypass.",
src/app/osw/content/mini-gastric-bypass/page.tsx:34:    "Compared with a standard Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, the mini bypass uses one join rather than two, which can mean a shorter operation and a simpler recovery, with comparable weight loss for most people.",
src/app/osw/content/mini-gastric-bypass/page.tsx:36:    "Generally suitable for patients with a BMI of 35+ or BMI 30+ with related health conditions.",
src/app/osw/content/mini-gastric-bypass/page.tsx:38:    "You have a BMI of 35 or above, or 30 or above with conditions such as type 2 diabetes, sleep apnoea or high blood pressure.",
src/app/osw/content/mini-gastric-bypass/page.tsx:93:    "Costs depend on your private health cover, your hospital and your individual situation, so we provide a clear written estimate before you commit to anything. Mini gastric bypass procedures attract a Medicare rebate. Your surgeon will confirm the applicable item number from the MBS bariatric surgery family (items 31569-31581) that applies to your specific procedure. Medicare and most private health funds with the relevant level of cover contribute to bariatric surgery, which reduces your out-of-pocket amount. Some patients use early release of superannuation to help fund treatment. Our team will walk you through the figures and the gaps so there are no surprises. Ask us for a personalised quote rather than relying on a general number.",
