src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:5:  title: "Medicare & Private Health for Weight Loss Surgery | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:23:    { label: "Hospital and theatre", value: "Not Medicare", note: "Funded through your private hospital cover, not Medicare" },
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:29:    "Funding weight loss surgery in Australia usually involves two separate systems working side by side: Medicare and your private health insurance. Medicare pays a set rebate toward eligible medical services, including the surgical item numbers commonly used for bariatric procedures, such as 31575 (sleeve gastrectomy), 31572 (Roux-en-Y bypass) and other items in the MBS bariatric surgery family (31569-31581), as well as the anaesthetist, physician and dietitian consultations attached to your care. Medicare does not, however, pay for your hospital stay, theatre fees or accommodation.",
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:30:    "That hospital side is where private health insurance comes in. To have your hospital and theatre costs covered, you generally need a gold tier policy, or an equivalent level that specifically includes bariatric or weight loss surgery and lists the relevant item numbers, including 31575 and 31572 among others. Not every gold policy includes bariatric surgery, and not every fund treats it the same way, which is exactly why checking your individual policy matters so much.",
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:34:    "Think of it this way: Medicare contributes to the doctors' fees, your private hospital cover handles the hospital and theatre, and the gap, excess or copayment is the part you fund yourself.",
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:36:    "Cover is generally relevant for patients with a BMI of 35 or above, or 30 or above with related health conditions, who are clinically suitable for surgery. Your fund may have its own criteria in addition to the clinical ones.",
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:39:    "Medicare does not cover hospital theatre fees or accommodation; that is the role of your private hospital cover.",
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:40:    "Private hospital cover generally needs to be gold tier or equivalent and must specifically include bariatric surgery item numbers including 31575 (sleeve gastrectomy) and 31572 (Roux-en-Y bypass).",
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:58:    "SJOG's agreed rates with most major funds can meaningfully reduce out-of-pocket costs compared with a hospital that has no such arrangement.",
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:69:    "Medicare rebates are set amounts and do not cover hospital theatre or accommodation, which can be a significant cost without suitable hospital cover.",
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:72:    "We do not publish a single fixed figure online because your real cost depends on your procedure, your private health cover, the Medicare rebates that apply and your fund's excess or copayment. Medicare contributes toward eligible item numbers including 31575 (sleeve gastrectomy), 31572 (Roux-en-Y bypass) and other items in the MBS bariatric surgery family (31569-31581), and toward your anaesthetist, physician and dietitian consultations, while your private hospital cover handles the hospital and theatre side. With St John of God's agreed rates across most major funds, many patients reduce their out-of-pocket costs considerably. The clearest way to understand your position is to have our financial coordinator review your cover and give you a written, itemised estimate before you decide.",
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:93:        "Medicare contributes a set rebate toward eligible medical services, including the surgical item numbers commonly used for bariatric surgery, such as 31575 (sleeve gastrectomy), 31572 (Roux-en-Y bypass) and other items in the MBS bariatric surgery family (31569-31581), and toward your anaesthetist, physician and dietitian consultations. Medicare does not pay for your hospital stay, theatre fees or accommodation, which is why private hospital cover is usually needed alongside it.",
src/app/osw/content/medicare-private-health-cover/page.tsx:123:        "Even with Medicare rebates and private hospital cover, most patients have some out-of-pocket cost. This can include a gap between the surgeon's or anaesthetist's fee and the combined Medicare and fund rebate, plus your policy excess or copayment. The exact amount depends on your cover, so we provide a written, itemised estimate rather than a single online figure.",
src/app/osw/content/post-weight-loss-skin-removal/page.tsx:5:  title: "Excess Skin Removal After Weight Loss (Panniculectomy) | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/post-weight-loss-skin-removal/page.tsx:25:    { label: "Where", value: "SJOG Murdoch & Subiaco", note: "Performed at St John of God private hospitals" },
src/app/osw/content/post-weight-loss-skin-removal/page.tsx:31:    "This is important to be clear about from the outset. At Obesity Surgery WA this procedure is assessed and offered strictly on medical grounds, to treat a functional problem caused by redundant skin. It is not offered as an appearance procedure, and eligibility is decided individually by your surgeon based on your symptoms and medical history.",
src/app/osw/content/post-weight-loss-skin-removal/page.tsx:70:        "If the procedure is medically indicated, the panniculectomy is performed at St John of God Murdoch or Subiaco. This is a major operation, and your surgeon will explain the approach, the expected scarring and your hospital stay beforehand.",
src/app/osw/content/post-weight-loss-skin-removal/page.tsx:88:    "We want to be direct with you, because this is major surgery and it carries real risks. A panniculectomy involves a large incision and the removal of a significant amount of tissue, which means a long scar and a recovery that takes time. This information is given out of respect for your decision, not to alarm you. Most patients recover well, but you deserve a clear picture before you decide, and your surgeon will talk through exactly how these risks apply to your situation.",
src/app/osw/content/post-weight-loss-skin-removal/page.tsx:99:    "Cost is a fair thing to ask about early. Because this procedure is offered only where it is medically indicated, the total depends on whether your case meets the relevant Medicare and private health fund criteria, your level of hospital cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, and the length of your hospital stay. Medicare and private health fund support applies only to medically justified cases that meet the criteria, and is assessed individually rather than being automatic. Rather than quote a single figure that may not apply to your situation, we provide a clear, itemised written estimate after your assessment, once it is known whether the procedure is medically indicated for you.",
src/app/osw/content/post-weight-loss-skin-removal/page.tsx:150:        "The procedure is performed at St John of God Murdoch or St John of God Subiaco, both well-equipped private hospitals. You will need a referral from your GP, ideally noting the skin problems you have experienced, which allows you to claim Medicare rebates on your consultations and is the starting point for being assessed. If you are unsure how to arrange the referral, our team can talk you through it.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-dietitian-perth/page.tsx:5:  title: "Bariatric Dietitian Perth | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-dietitian-perth/page.tsx:36:    "Dietitian support is part of bariatric care for patients generally suitable for surgery, typically a BMI of 35+, or BMI 30+ with related health conditions.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-dietitian-perth/page.tsx:139:        "Yes. All dietitians at Obesity Surgery WA hold Accredited Practising Dietitian (APD) status, which is the professional standard required for clinical practice in Australia. Our team includes Clare Renn, Megan Parker and Rhiannon Dick, all of whom specialise in bariatric and metabolic nutrition support.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-dietitian-perth/page.tsx:142:      question: "How many dietitians does Obesity Surgery WA have?",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-band/page.tsx:5:  title: "Gastric Band Surgery Perth | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-band/page.tsx:7:    "Gastric band surgery in Perth at Obesity Surgery WA. Adjustable laparoscopic band: how it works, who it suits, and how it compares to sleeve and bypass.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-band/page.tsx:17:    "Gastric band surgery in Perth at Obesity Surgery WA. Adjustable laparoscopic band: how it works, who it suits, and how it compares to sleeve and bypass.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-band/page.tsx:35:  eligibilityBmi: "Often considered for patients with a BMI between 30 and 40, particularly where reversibility is a priority.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-band/page.tsx:38:    "It can be a reasonable choice at a lower BMI, in the 30 to 40 range, where a sleeve or bypass may be more than is needed.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-band/page.tsx:41:    "Eligibility is always a clinical decision made with you. Your overall health, your goals and your readiness to commit to follow-up all matter, not just your BMI.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-band/page.tsx:80:    "A shorter hospital stay and generally quicker initial recovery than a sleeve or bypass.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-band/page.tsx:93:    "General surgical risks such as bleeding, infection, blood clots or a reaction to anaesthetic.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-band/page.tsx:96:    "Most patients ask about cost early, and it is a fair question. The total depends on your private health cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, your hospital stay and the ongoing adjustment visits the band requires. Gastric band procedures are listed under Medicare item number 31569. Medicare and most private health funds with appropriate hospital cover contribute towards bariatric surgery, and some patients access their superannuation through early release to help with out-of-pocket costs. Rather than quote a single figure that may not apply to you, we give every patient a clear, itemised written estimate before any decision is made, so there are no surprises.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:5:  title: "Gastric Sleeve Perth | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:7:    "Gastric sleeve surgery in Perth at Obesity Surgery WA, led by Dr Harsha Chandraratna FRACS. Eligibility, costs, recovery and long-term outcomes explained.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:17:    "Gastric sleeve surgery in Perth at Obesity Surgery WA, led by Dr Harsha Chandraratna FRACS. Eligibility, costs, recovery and long-term outcomes explained.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:56:    "Generally suitable for adults with a BMI of 35 or above, or a BMI of 30 to 35 with weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, sleep apnoea or high blood pressure.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:58:    "BMI is only a starting point. Your surgeon assesses your whole health picture, not just a number on a chart.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:82:      title: "Hospital recovery",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:104:    "A keyhole approach, which generally means smaller scars, less post-operative pain and a shorter hospital stay than open surgery.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:117:    "General surgical risks including bleeding, infection, blood clots and reactions to anaesthetic, as with any operation.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:121:    "The total cost of a gastric sleeve in Perth depends on your private health cover, your surgeon's and anaesthetist's fees, hospital charges and any out-of-pocket gap. We believe in being upfront about money, because uncertainty about cost is one of the biggest sources of stress for people considering surgery. The gastric sleeve is listed under Medicare item number 31575, which attracts a Medicare rebate when you meet the eligibility criteria. Our cost guide breaks down the components and the funding options available, including private health insurance and early access to superannuation in eligible cases.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:163:      question: "What does recovery actually look like week to week?",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:165:        "Most people stay in hospital for around two nights. You will be on fluids at first, moving to pureed and then soft foods over the following weeks under dietitian guidance. Many return to desk work after about two weeks, while physical or manual jobs usually need four to six weeks. It is normal to feel tired in the early weeks as your body adjusts, and our team checks in with you throughout rather than leaving you to figure it out alone.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-sleeve-perth/page.tsx:175:        "Yes, the gastric sleeve is listed under Medicare item number 31575. Medicare pays a set rebate toward the surgeon's, anaesthetist's and certain physician fees. It does not cover the hospital and theatre component, which is funded through your private health cover. You generally need a gold-tier or equivalent private hospital policy that specifically includes bariatric surgery.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:5:  title: "Bariatric Surgeons Perth | Obesity Surgery WA",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:7:    "Meet the Perth bariatric surgeons at Obesity Surgery WA: Dr Harsha Chandraratna (FRACS) and Dr Stephen Watson, plus our physicians and dietitians.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:17:    "Meet the Perth bariatric surgeons at Obesity Surgery WA: Dr Harsha Chandraratna (FRACS) and Dr Stephen Watson, plus our physicians and dietitians.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:27:  whatIsTitle: "Who are the surgeons at Obesity Surgery WA?",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:29:    "Obesity Surgery WA is led by Dr Harsha Chandraratna, a bariatric surgeon and Fellow of the Royal Australasian College of Surgeons (FRACS). He lectures at the University of Notre Dame and operates at St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco, two of Perth's leading private hospitals. He has also been through bariatric surgery himself, which means he understands the experience from both sides of the consulting room.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:34:    "When you compare bariatric surgeons in Perth, look beyond a single number. The things that matter most are college fellowship (FRACS), accredited hospital privileges, the strength of the aftercare team, and whether the surgeon takes time to understand your situation.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:36:    "Generally suitable for patients with a BMI of 35+ or BMI 30+ with related health conditions. Your surgeon will confirm what is right for you.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:39:    "Both surgeons hold operating privileges at St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco, accredited private hospitals.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:47:        "Ask your GP for a referral to Obesity Surgery WA. This gives you access to Medicare rebates and lets us review your history before we meet.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:67:        "You recover in hospital under the care of our team, usually for a few nights, with nursing and dietitian support before you head home.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:79:    "Operating privileges at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco, with full hospital theatre and intensive-care backup if it is ever needed.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:93:    "Costs depend on the procedure you and your surgeon decide on, your level of private health cover, and your individual circumstances. Most bariatric procedures attract a Medicare item number, and patients with appropriate private hospital cover often pay an out-of-pocket gap rather than the full fee. Some people choose to access their superannuation early to fund surgery. We give you a clear written estimate before you commit to anything, so there are no surprises.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:99:        "Lead surgeon and founder of Obesity Surgery WA. Fellow of the Royal Australasian College of Surgeons and lecturer at the University of Notre Dame. Operates at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco, with personal experience of bariatric surgery that shapes his patient-centred approach.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:114:        "Look for a surgeon who is a Fellow of the Royal Australasian College of Surgeons (FRACS), holds operating privileges at accredited hospitals, performs bariatric surgery regularly, and is backed by a proper aftercare team of dietitians and physicians. Just as important is how the surgeon treats you. A good one takes time to understand your situation and explains your options honestly rather than rushing you toward a decision.",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:132:      question: "Which hospitals do the surgeons operate at?",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:134:        "Our surgeons operate at St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco, two accredited private hospitals in Perth with full theatre and recovery facilities. Consultations are held at our Booragoon rooms at Garden City Specialist Centre (537 Marmion Street) and in Mandurah at WA Cardiology Centre (30 Minilya Pkwy).",
src/app/osw/content/surgeons/page.tsx:154:        "Most patients take around two to four weeks off, depending on the procedure and how physical your job is. Office-based work is often possible sooner, while heavy lifting needs longer. Your surgeon will give you advice tailored to your situation and your recovery during your consultation.",
src/app/osw/content/page.tsx:12:  { group: "Core acquisition", slug: "gastric-bypass-perth", title: "Gastric Bypass Perth", priority: "P0" },
src/app/osw/content/page.tsx:31:  { group: "Trust & E-E-A-T", slug: "hospitals-locations", title: "Hospitals & Clinic Locations", priority: "P0" },
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:5:  title: "Weight Loss Surgery Cost Perth | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:6:  description: "Weight loss surgery costs in Perth: surgeon fees, hospital fees, private health insurance, Medicare rebates, super access and payment options.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:16:    "Weight loss surgery costs in Perth: surgeon fees, hospital fees, private health insurance, Medicare rebates, super access and payment options.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:32:    "The main components are the surgeon's fee, the anaesthetist's fee, the assistant surgeon's fee where applicable, and the hospital fee. On top of those, there can be costs for the dietitian, follow up appointments and any tests required before surgery. Each of these is billed separately, which is part of why the headline number can feel confusing.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:34:    "The single biggest factor in what you actually pay is whether you have appropriate private health insurance and whether you have served the waiting period. With the right cover, the hospital fee is largely taken care of and your out of pocket drops considerably. Without it, the hospital component becomes a significant cost you carry yourself.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:37:    "A note on language: every range on this page uses approximately or around on purpose. Costs move with the procedure, your surgeon's assessment, your insurer's policy and the hospital. We do not ask you to make a decision on a ballpark. We give you a written estimate first.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:40:    "Medicare rebates for bariatric surgery generally apply where surgery is clinically indicated, which usually means a BMI of 35 or above, or 30 and above with an obesity related health condition. Your eligibility for funding is assessed alongside your clinical eligibility.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:42:    "Private health insurance is the main path most patients use to fund surgery. You generally need gold tier hospital cover, or a top level of cover that explicitly includes the obesity surgery item numbers.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:43:    "The relevant Medicare item numbers for bariatric procedures include 31575 (sleeve) and 31572 (bypass). Check that your hospital policy covers these specific numbers, not just a general weight management category.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:45:    "Medicare provides a rebate on the surgical and consultation fees for eligible patients, which reduces the surgeon and anaesthetist out of pocket. Medicare does not cover the hospital admission for elective bariatric surgery.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:64:        "Before you commit to anything, we give you a personalised estimate that lists the surgeon, anaesthetist, assistant and hospital fees, what your insurer is expected to cover, and your likely out of pocket. No surprises is the standard we hold ourselves to.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:92:    "Assuming all private health policies cover obesity surgery. Many do not, and some cover the consultation but not the hospital item numbers. Always check 31575 (sleeve) and 31572 (bypass) specifically.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:93:    "Overlooking the 12 month waiting period. Booking surgery before the waiting period is served can mean the hospital fee falls entirely on you.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:94:    "Forgetting that Medicare does not cover the hospital admission. The Medicare rebate applies to surgical and consultation fees, not the hospital stay.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:101:    "The honest summary is this: with appropriate private health cover and the waiting period served, your out of pocket for a gastric sleeve is typically around $12,000 to $20,000, and a gastric bypass around $15,000 to $25,000, with banding generally lower. Without suitable cover, the hospital fee adds a significant amount on top. Medicare rebates reduce the surgical and consultation portion for eligible patients but do not cover the hospital admission. Some providers offer payment plans, and super may be accessible on compassionate grounds where obesity is causing a serious medical condition. None of this needs to be worked out in your head. Our team prepares a written estimate specific to you so you can see the real numbers before deciding anything.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:119:    "Patients consistently tell us that the part they valued most was knowing the full cost up front. When the surgeon fee, anaesthetist fee, hospital fee and insurance rebates are all laid out on one page before any decision is made, the financial stress drops and people can focus on their health. Weight loss results themselves vary between individuals and depend on the procedure, your health factors and your commitment to the recommended dietary and lifestyle changes. What we can commit to is transparency on every dollar.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:130:        "To have the hospital component of bariatric surgery covered, you generally need gold tier hospital cover or a top level policy that explicitly includes the obesity surgery item numbers, commonly 31575 (sleeve) and 31572 (bypass). Most funds apply a 12 month waiting period for obesity surgery as a pre existing condition. We strongly recommend checking your policy covers those specific item numbers, not just a general weight management category, and confirming you have served the waiting period before booking.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:135:        "Medicare provides a rebate on the surgical and consultation fees for eligible patients, which reduces the surgeon and anaesthetist out of pocket. Importantly, Medicare does not cover the hospital admission for elective bariatric surgery. That hospital fee is either covered by private health insurance or paid out of pocket. Eligibility for the Medicare rebate generally aligns with clinical indication, usually a BMI of 35 and above, or 30 and above with an obesity related health condition.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:150:        "Bariatric surgery involves a surgeon, an anaesthetist, often an assistant surgeon, and a hospital, and each bills separately. There can also be costs for the dietitian, pre operative tests and follow up appointments. This is standard across surgical care, but it is why a single headline number can be misleading. Our written estimate lists every component so you can see precisely what each fee is for and what your insurer is expected to cover.",
src/app/osw/content/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth/page.tsx:155:        "Book a consultation with us. After your surgeon assesses which procedure is appropriate, our team checks your private health cover and Medicare eligibility, then prepares a written, itemised estimate showing the surgeon, anaesthetist, assistant and hospital fees, the expected insurer contribution and your likely out of pocket. You will have the full picture before you commit to anything. No surprises is the standard we hold ourselves to.",
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:5:  title: "Gallbladder Removal Perth (Laparoscopic Cholecystectomy) | Obesity Surgery WA",
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:7:    "Gallbladder removal in Perth by keyhole (laparoscopic cholecystectomy). What the gallbladder does, gallstones after rapid weight loss, recovery and cost at Obesity Surgery WA.",
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:17:    "Gallbladder removal in Perth by keyhole (laparoscopic cholecystectomy). What the gallbladder does, gallstones after rapid weight loss, recovery and cost at Obesity Surgery WA.",
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:25:    { label: "Where", value: "SJOG Murdoch & Subiaco", note: "Performed at St John of God private hospitals" },
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:31:    "The operation itself is almost always done by keyhole, or laparoscopic, surgery. Rather than one large cut, the surgeon makes a few small incisions and uses a tiny camera and fine instruments to remove the gallbladder, along with any stones inside it. Keyhole surgery generally means less pain and a quicker recovery than the older open operation, and most people go home the same day or after one night.",
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:36:    "There is no BMI requirement for gallbladder surgery. The decision to remove the gallbladder is based on whether gallstones are causing you symptoms or complications, not on your weight.",
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:69:        "Most people go home the same day or after one night in hospital. You will be given clear advice on pain relief, wound care and what to eat in the first few days.",
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:74:        "Many people feel close to normal within one to two weeks, though full recovery can take a little longer. A follow-up appointment lets your surgeon check your healing and answer any questions about life without a gallbladder.",
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:91:    "General surgical risks such as bleeding, wound infection and blood clots.",
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:96:    "Cost is a fair thing to ask about early. The final figure depends on your private health cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, the hospital, and how long you stay. Medicare and most private health funds with appropriate hospital cover contribute towards gallbladder surgery when it is clinically indicated, and your eligibility for rebates depends on your individual circumstances. Rather than quote a single number that may not apply to your case, we give every patient a clear, itemised written estimate before any decision is made, so you can see exactly what is involved and what you may pay out of pocket.",
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:132:        "Most people go home the same day or after one night, and feel close to normal within one to two weeks, though full recovery can take a little longer. You will have some discomfort around the small wounds at first, which settles. Your surgeon will advise on returning to work and activity, which depends on your job and how you are healing, rather than a fixed timeline.",
src/app/osw/content/gallbladder-removal-perth/page.tsx:147:        "Gallbladder removal is performed at St John of God Murdoch or St John of God Subiaco, both well-equipped private hospitals. You will need a referral from your GP, which allows you to claim Medicare rebates on your consultations and is the starting point for seeing one of our surgeons. If you are unsure how to arrange the referral, our team can talk you through it when you call.",
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:6:  Quote: "Medicare item numbers 31572 and 31581 attract a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, and private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) covers the hospital and theatre component."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:7:  Suggested rewrite: "Medicare rebates may apply when MBS criteria are met. For Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, the relevant MBS item is 31572; your surgeon will confirm the appropriate Medicare item number, rebate eligibility and any private health fund requirements before surgery. Private health insurance with appropriate hospital cover may contribute to hospital and theatre costs, but out-of-pocket costs vary by fund and policy."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:9:- Finding: The BMI eligibility sentence is broader than current MBS wording and could mislead readers about Medicare eligibility. MBS explanatory note TN.8.29 generally describes clinically severe obesity as BMI 40 or higher, or BMI 35 or higher with major medical comorbidities, with clinical judgment and population differences noted.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:10:  Quote: "A gastric bypass is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30–35 with associated weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:11:  Suggested rewrite: "Gastric bypass suitability is assessed individually. For Medicare-funded bariatric surgery, clinically severe obesity is generally described as a BMI of 40 or higher, or a BMI of 35 or higher with major medical comorbidities, although your surgeon will consider your full health picture and whether any population-specific or clinical factors apply. Some lower BMI ranges may be discussed for selected metabolic-surgery patients, but Medicare rebate eligibility must be confirmed by the surgical team."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:22:  Quote: "The small keyhole incisions mean less pain post-operatively, smaller scars, and generally, a faster recovery than open surgery, though your surgeon will discuss your goals and your different surgical options during your initial consultation."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:23:  Suggested rewrite: "Compared with open surgery, keyhole surgery may reduce wound size and support recovery for suitable patients, but pain, scarring and recovery time vary and complications can still occur. Your surgeon will confirm whether a keyhole approach is appropriate for you."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:37:  Suggested rewrite: "Gastric bypass Perth: surgery, cost, recovery and suitability"
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:53:  Suggested rewrite: "Expected weight loss: 70-80% of excess weight, clinical average; results vary. Time in surgery: about 120 minutes under general anaesthetic using a keyhole technique. Hospital stay: usually 2-3 nights. Return to most activity: about 3-4 weeks, with light duties often sooner and heavy lifting later."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:55:- Finding: The current hospital locations conflict with the brief, which says St John of God Murdoch or Subiaco and asks for St John of God Murdoch as a semantic term.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:56:  Quote: "After your bypass you’ll spend 2-3 nights recuperating at [<u>Mount Private Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [<u>Waikiki Private Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:57:  Suggested rewrite: "After your bypass, you will usually spend 2-3 nights recovering in hospital at St John of God Murdoch or Subiaco, depending on your surgeon, theatre availability and clinical needs."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:65:  Suggested rewrite: "Rather than quoting one figure that may not apply to your circumstances, the practice provides a clear written estimate before you make a decision. This estimate explains surgeon, anaesthetist, hospital, Medicare and private health fund components, plus any expected out-of-pocket gap."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:83:- Finding: The FAQ set covers the brief's eight questions, but it misses refreshed PAA questions for the 30/30 rule, Medicare coverage and cost in Perth.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:85:  Suggested rewrite: "FAQs (10 questions): keep the current brief FAQs, but replace the loose skin FAQ or shorten the lower-priority questions to add: 'What is the 30/30 rule after gastric bypass?', 'Can gastric bypass be covered by Medicare?', and 'How much does gastric bypass cost in Perth?'"
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:87:- Finding: Add a short 30/30 rule FAQ because the refresh says it appears in bypass/bariatric PAA.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:88:  Quote: Missing - no draft sentence answers the 30/30 rule for gastric bypass.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:89:  Suggested rewrite: "The 30/30 rule usually means avoiding fluids for about 30 minutes before and after meals so your small stomach pouch is not overfilled and you can focus on slow, mindful eating. Your dietitian will confirm the timing that is right for you after surgery."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:93:  Suggested rewrite: "Medicare may contribute to gastric bypass when MBS criteria are met, but it does not usually cover every cost. Your surgeon will confirm whether an MBS item applies, and your private health fund can confirm hospital-cover requirements and any waiting periods."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:97:  Suggested rewrite: "The cost of gastric bypass in Perth depends on your surgeon, anaesthetist, hospital, private health cover and Medicare eligibility. OSWA provides an itemised written estimate before surgery so you can see likely out-of-pocket costs before deciding."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:111:- Finding: The BMI tool prompt should be an internal link to the eligibility page, not a dead instruction.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:112:  Quote: "Calculate your BMI."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:113:  Suggested rewrite: "Use our am I eligible for weight loss surgery guide to calculate your BMI and understand the other factors your surgeon will consider."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:126:  Quote: "You may need to follow a specific pre-op diet to shrink your liver and reduce surgical risk (typically 2–4 weeks), but you won’t do it alone!"
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:127:  Suggested rewrite: "You may need to follow a specific pre-operative diet for 2-4 weeks to reduce liver size and surgical risk, with guidance from the OSWA dietitian team."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:135:  Suggested rewrite: "Many patients return to light daily activities gradually over the first few weeks, with return to work and heavier activity depending on the type of work, recovery progress and surgeon advice."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:154:  Quote: "Pre- and post-operative (once you’ve been discharged from hospital) consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre, Booragoon, and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:157:Summary: The draft has the right broad structure and a useful bypass-vs-sleeve angle, but it is not ready to publish because Medicare/BMI wording, item-number accuracy and risk disclosure need correction.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:169:- **Blocking "keyhole less pain" — downgraded to Nice-to-have. BRIEF-INHERITED:** the draft is a near-verbatim implementation of `content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md:86`, including the surgeon-confirms qualifier the brief asks for.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:170:- **Blocking #2 (BMI wording) — BRIEF-INHERITED.** `content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md:93` mandates "BMI of 35 or above, or BMI 30 or above with related health conditions." MBS-accuracy concern stands; fix draft AND brief.
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:5:  title: "Bariatric Surgery Perth | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:6:  description: "Bariatric surgery in Perth at Obesity Surgery WA. Gastric sleeve, bypass, band and medical weight management from a dedicated Perth bariatric team.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:16:    "Bariatric surgery in Perth at Obesity Surgery WA. Gastric sleeve, bypass, band and medical weight management from a dedicated Perth bariatric team.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:20:    "If you have been carrying excess weight for years and feel like you have already tried everything, you are not alone, and you are not out of options. Obesity Surgery WA offers gastric sleeve, gastric bypass, mini bypass, gastric band, revision surgery and medical weight management, all delivered by a dedicated Perth bariatric team across St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:33:    "Most bariatric procedures today are performed using keyhole (laparoscopic) techniques, which means smaller incisions, less pain, and a quicker recovery than older open surgery. At Obesity Surgery WA, the choice of procedure is never one size fits all. Your surgeon will recommend an approach based on your weight, your health conditions, your eating patterns and what you want your life to look like afterwards.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:39:    "Bariatric surgery is generally considered for adults with a BMI of 40 or above, or a BMI of 35 or above when an obesity-related health condition is also present. People with a BMI between 30 and 35 may be candidates in specific circumstances, particularly where type 2 diabetes is poorly controlled.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:41:    "BMI of 40 or above: surgery is generally an appropriate option to discuss, regardless of other conditions.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:42:    "BMI of 35 to 40 with a related condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea or joint disease: surgery is commonly considered.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:43:    "BMI of 30 to 35: surgery may be considered in select cases, often where diabetes is difficult to manage with medication alone. Your eligibility is assessed individually.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:46:    "Final eligibility is always determined after a full assessment with one of our surgeons and the wider team, including review of your medical history and any conditions that affect surgical risk.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:68:        "This often includes a specific pre-operative diet to shrink the liver and make surgery safer, along with practical guidance on what to expect on the day and during early recovery.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:73:        "Surgery is performed laparoscopically in most cases, at St John of God Murdoch or Subiaco. Hospital stays are typically short, and your surgeon will explain your individual recovery plan.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:96:    "General surgical risks such as bleeding, infection, blood clots and reaction to anaesthetic, as with any operation.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:105:    "Cost is one of the first things on most people's minds, and it is a fair question. The total cost of bariatric surgery in Perth depends on your procedure, your private health cover, your level of excess (out of pocket) and the hospital and anaesthetic fees involved. Many patients with appropriate private health insurance find that a significant portion is covered, and some access their superannuation to help fund treatment. Our team will give you a clear, itemised breakdown before you commit to anything, with no pressure and no surprises.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:118:        "Accredited Bariatric Surgeon performing gastric sleeve, gastric bypass and gastric band procedures at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco. Dr Watson works closely with the wider Obesity Surgery WA team to match each patient with the procedure best suited to their health and goals.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:129:        "There is no single best procedure for everyone. The right choice depends on your BMI, your eating patterns, your health conditions, particularly whether you have type 2 diabetes, how much weight loss you are aiming for, and your own preferences around reversibility and lifestyle. Your surgeon will talk through the realistic pros and cons of the sleeve, bypass, mini bypass and band for your specific situation. The goal of your consultation is to land on the option that fits your life, not to push you toward any one operation.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:134:        "Bariatric surgery is recognised under Medicare item numbers for eligible patients, and most procedures are performed in the private setting through hospitals such as St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco. The portion covered depends on your private health policy, your level of cover and any waiting periods. Our team will help you understand the Medicare items that apply, what your insurer is likely to cover, and your expected out of pocket cost before you make any decision.",
src/app/osw/content/bariatric-surgery-perth/page.tsx:154:        "Our surgeons operate at St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco, both well-established private hospitals in Perth with strong surgical and recovery facilities. Consultations, assessments and aftercare appointments are coordinated through our Perth-based team, so you are looked after locally throughout your journey, from your first consultation through to long-term follow-up.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:5:  title: "Gastric Bypass Surgery Perth | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:7:    "Roux-en-Y gastric bypass in Perth with Dr Harsha Chandraratna. Often chosen for type 2 diabetes. Eligibility, recovery, risks and costs explained clearly.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:8:  alternates: { canonical: "https://www.obesitysurgerywa.com.au/gastric-bypass-perth" },
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:13:  slug: "gastric-bypass-perth",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:17:    "Roux-en-Y gastric bypass in Perth with Dr Harsha Chandraratna. Often chosen for type 2 diabetes. Eligibility, recovery, risks and costs explained clearly.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:22:    { label: "Expected weight loss", value: "70-80%", note: "Excess weight, clinical average. Results vary between individuals" },
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:31:    "The whole operation is done through several small keyhole incisions rather than one large cut. This usually means less pain after surgery, smaller scars and a quicker recovery than open surgery. Your surgeon will confirm during your consultation whether keyhole is suitable for you.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:35:  eligibilityBmi: "Generally suitable for patients with a BMI of 35+ or BMI 30+ with related health conditions.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:40:    "Eligibility is always a clinical decision made with you. Your overall health, history of weight-loss attempts and personal goals all matter, not just your BMI.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:76:    "Significant, durable weight loss: clinical averages show patients lose around 70-80% of their excess weight, though individual results vary.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:86:    "General surgical risks such as bleeding, infection, blood clots or a reaction to anaesthetic.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:87:    "A small risk of a leak at one of the new connections, which is why we monitor you closely in hospital.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:92:    "Most patients ask about cost early, and it is a fair question. The total depends on your private health cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, and your hospital stay. Medicare and most private health funds with appropriate hospital cover contribute towards bariatric surgery, and some patients access their superannuation through early release to help with out-of-pocket costs. Rather than quote a single figure that may not apply to you, we give every patient a clear, itemised written estimate before any decision is made, so there are no surprises.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:104:        "Experienced bariatric surgeon performing keyhole gastric bypass and other weight-loss procedures across our Perth hospitals.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:113:        "On average, patients lose around 70-80% of their excess weight, with the fastest loss in the first six to twelve months. These are clinical averages and your own result will depend on your starting point and the lifestyle changes you make. We set realistic, personal goals with you rather than a one-size-fits-all number.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:123:        "Most patients take around two to three weeks off, though it depends on your job. If you have a desk-based role you may return sooner, while physical work or heavy lifting usually needs three to four weeks or more. We will give you specific advice based on your recovery and what you do for a living.",
src/app/osw/content/gastric-bypass-perth/page.tsx:148:        "Surgery takes place at St John of God Murdoch or St John of God Subiaco, both well-equipped private hospitals. Our clinic consultations are held in Booragoon at the Garden City Specialist Centre and in Mandurah at the WA Cardiology Centre.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:5:  title: "Hospitals & Clinic Locations | Obesity Surgery WA",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:7:    "Where Obesity Surgery WA operates and consults: surgery at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco, with consulting rooms in Booragoon (Perth) and Mandurah.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:8:  alternates: { canonical: "https://www.obesitysurgerywa.com.au/hospitals-locations" },
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:13:  slug: "hospitals-locations",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:15:  keyword: "bariatric surgery hospital perth",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:17:    "Where Obesity Surgery WA operates and consults: surgery at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco, with consulting rooms in Booragoon (Perth) and Mandurah.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:20:    "Knowing where your surgery will happen, and where you will be seen before and after, is a reasonable thing to want settled early. Obesity Surgery WA performs surgery at two accredited private hospitals in Perth, St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco, and consults from rooms in Booragoon and Mandurah so you can be seen closer to home.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:22:    { label: "Surgical hospitals", value: "2", note: "St John of God Murdoch & Subiaco" },
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:24:    { label: "Hospital type", value: "Private", note: "Accredited private hospitals" },
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:27:  whatIsTitle: "Where Obesity Surgery WA operates and consults",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:29:    "Surgery is performed at St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco. Both are accredited private hospitals in Perth with full operating theatres, recovery wards and intensive-care backup on site. Which hospital your procedure is booked at depends on your surgeon's operating lists and your circumstances; your surgeon and our team confirm the location with you well before the day.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:30:    "Most of your appointments, before and after surgery, happen in our consulting rooms rather than at the hospital. We consult from Booragoon at the Garden City Specialist Centre, 537 Marmion Street, which is our main Perth rooms. We also hold consultations in Mandurah at the WA Cardiology Centre, 30 Minilya Pkwy, so patients south of the city do not have to travel into Perth for every visit.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:31:    "It helps to think of it as two kinds of location. The consulting rooms in Booragoon and Mandurah are where you meet your surgeon, have your assessment, and return for follow-up. The hospitals at Murdoch and Subiaco are where the operation itself takes place and where you recover in the days afterwards. One phone number, (08) 9332 0066, covers all of them.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:34:    "When you are weighing up where to have surgery, the things that matter most are whether it is an accredited private hospital, whether there is a full operating theatre and intensive-care backup on the same site, and how easily you can get to your follow-up appointments. Murdoch and Subiaco are both accredited private hospitals with that backup; consulting in Booragoon or Mandurah keeps your routine visits close to home.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:37:    "Consultations are held in Booragoon (Garden City Specialist Centre, 537 Marmion Street) and Mandurah (WA Cardiology Centre, 30 Minilya Pkwy), not at the hospital.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:38:    "Surgery and your in-hospital recovery take place at St John of God Murdoch or St John of God Subiaco, both accredited private hospitals.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:39:    "Both hospitals are private, so appropriate private hospital cover (or a self-funded arrangement) is relevant; our team explains your options before anything is booked.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:46:        "Ask your GP for a referral to Obesity Surgery WA. This gives you access to Medicare rebates and lets us review your history before we meet.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:66:        "You recover in hospital under the care of our team, usually for a few nights, with nursing and dietitian support before you head home.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:76:    "Surgery at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco, two accredited private hospitals with full theatre, recovery and intensive-care backup on site.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:84:    "Choosing an accredited private hospital with full theatre and intensive-care backup is part of having surgery done carefully, but no hospital or surgeon can remove the risks that surgery carries. We would rather be straight with you about that than imply otherwise. The location is chosen to give you a well-equipped, accredited setting; it does not change the honest fact that any operation has risks.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:92:    "Because surgery is performed at private hospitals, your costs depend on the procedure you and your surgeon decide on, your level of private health cover, and your individual circumstances. Most bariatric procedures attract a Medicare item number, and patients with appropriate private hospital cover often pay an out-of-pocket gap rather than the full fee. Some people choose to access their superannuation early to fund surgery. We give you a clear written estimate before you commit to anything, so there are no surprises.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:98:        "Lead surgeon and founder of Obesity Surgery WA. Fellow of the Royal Australasian College of Surgeons and lecturer at the University of Notre Dame. Operates at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco, with personal experience of bariatric surgery that shapes his patient-centred approach.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:113:        "Surgery is performed at St John of God Murdoch or St John of God Subiaco, two accredited private hospitals in Perth with full operating theatres, recovery wards and intensive-care backup on site. Your surgeon and our team confirm which hospital your procedure is booked at well before the day.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:118:        "Consultations happen in our rooms, not at the hospital. Our main Perth rooms are in Booragoon at the Garden City Specialist Centre, 537 Marmion Street. We also consult in Mandurah at the WA Cardiology Centre, 30 Minilya Pkwy. You meet your surgeon and return for follow-up at whichever location suits you.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:126:      question: "What is the difference between the consulting rooms and the hospital?",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:128:        "The consulting rooms in Booragoon and Mandurah are where you meet your surgeon, have your assessment, and return for follow-up appointments. The hospitals at Murdoch and Subiaco are where the operation takes place and where you recover in the days afterwards. Most of your appointments happen in the consulting rooms.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:131:      question: "Which hospital will my surgery be at, Murdoch or Subiaco?",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:133:        "It depends on your surgeon's operating lists and your individual circumstances. Both St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco are accredited private hospitals with the same standard of theatre and recovery facilities. Your surgeon and our team confirm the location with you well before the day so there are no surprises.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:136:      question: "Is parking available at the consulting rooms and hospitals?",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:138:        "Both St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco are full private hospital campuses with on-site patient parking. The Garden City Specialist Centre in Booragoon and the WA Cardiology Centre in Mandurah also have parking for patients. If you have specific access needs, mention them when you book on (08) 9332 0066 and our team will help.",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:141:      question: "Are these public or private hospitals?",
src/app/osw/content/hospitals-locations/page.tsx:143:        "St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco are both accredited private hospitals. That is why your level of private health cover, or a self-funded arrangement, is relevant to what surgery will cost. Our team explains your options clearly before anything is booked, and most bariatric procedures attract a Medicare item number.",
src/app/osw/content/revision-bariatric-surgery/page.tsx:5:  title: "Revision Bariatric Surgery Perth | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/revision-bariatric-surgery/page.tsx:7:    "Revision bariatric surgery Perth: sleeve to bypass, band removal, bypass revision. Expert care at Obesity Surgery WA when your first procedure hasn't lasted.",
src/app/osw/content/revision-bariatric-surgery/page.tsx:17:    "Revision bariatric surgery Perth: sleeve to bypass, band removal, bypass revision. Expert care at Obesity Surgery WA when your first procedure hasn't lasted.",
src/app/osw/content/revision-bariatric-surgery/page.tsx:25:    { label: "Where", value: "SJOG Murdoch & Subiaco", note: "Performed at St John of God private hospitals" },
src/app/osw/content/revision-bariatric-surgery/page.tsx:36:    "There is no single BMI cut-off for revision surgery. Suitability depends far more on what your first operation was, what has gone wrong, and your overall health than on a number alone.",
src/app/osw/content/revision-bariatric-surgery/page.tsx:90:    "An increased risk of leaks at the surgical connections, which is why monitoring in hospital is especially careful.",
src/app/osw/content/revision-bariatric-surgery/page.tsx:91:    "General surgical risks such as bleeding, infection, blood clots or a reaction to anaesthetic, which can be greater than the first time.",
src/app/osw/content/revision-bariatric-surgery/page.tsx:92:    "Often a longer operation and a longer recovery than the original procedure.",
src/app/osw/content/revision-bariatric-surgery/page.tsx:96:    "Cost is a fair thing to ask about early, and revision surgery is often more involved than a first procedure, which can affect the total. The final figure depends on your private health cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, and the length of your hospital stay. Medicare and most private health funds with appropriate hospital cover contribute towards eligible bariatric surgery, and some patients access their superannuation through early release to help with out-of-pocket costs. Rather than quote a single number that may not apply to your case, we give every patient a clear, itemised written estimate before any decision is made.",
src/app/osw/content/revision-bariatric-surgery/page.tsx:147:        "Revision procedures are performed at St John of God Murdoch or St John of God Subiaco, both well-equipped private hospitals. You will need a referral from your GP, which allows you to claim Medicare rebates on your consultations and is the starting point for seeing one of our surgeons. If you are unsure how to arrange the referral, our team can talk you through it when you call.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:5:  title: "Am I Eligible for Weight Loss Surgery? | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:7:    "Find out if you are eligible for weight loss surgery in Perth. BMI guidelines, health conditions, the assessment process and your first OSWA appointment.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:17:    "Find out if you are eligible for weight loss surgery in Perth. BMI guidelines, health conditions, the assessment process and your first OSWA appointment.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:21:    "If you have spent years trying to lose weight and keep ending up back where you started, you are not alone, and it is not a question of willpower. This page walks you through who qualifies for weight loss surgery in Perth, what your BMI and health history mean, and exactly what happens when you come in to talk to us. There is no obligation, and no pressure.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:24:    { label: "BMI 35+", value: "Usually eligible", note: "Most people at BMI 35 or over qualify for a surgical assessment" },
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:31:    "Most people start by asking whether their BMI is high enough. It is a fair question, and BMI does form part of the picture. But eligibility for weight loss surgery is not decided by one number on a chart. Your surgeon looks at your weight, your health conditions, the history of what you have already tried, and what you want your life to look like in five or ten years.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:32:    "Body Mass Index is your weight in kilograms divided by your height in metres squared. As a rough guide: BMI of 27 to 29 sits in the overweight range, 30 to 34 is class 1 obesity, 35 to 39 is class 2, and 40 and over is class 3, sometimes called severe obesity. Where you fall changes which options are likely to suit you, but it does not tell the whole story on its own.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:33:    "Two people can have the same BMI and end up with very different recommendations. Someone at BMI 32 with type 2 diabetes and sleep apnoea may be a strong candidate for surgery, while someone at the same BMI with no other health concerns might do well starting with our medical and dietitian-led program first. That is the reason an in-person assessment matters so much.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:36:    "If you are not sure of your BMI, do not worry about calculating it precisely before you call. Our team can help you work it out, and your surgeon will measure and confirm it at your consultation.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:39:    "As a general guide, people with a BMI of 35 or above are typically eligible for a surgical assessment, and those at 40 or above are almost always eligible. A BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify when weight-related health conditions are present.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:41:    "BMI 27 to 29: surgery is not usually the first step. Our medical team can often help with weight loss medication and dietitian support, which may be the right starting point for you.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:42:    "BMI 30 to 34 with a weight-related health condition: you may qualify for surgery. The presence of conditions such as type 2 diabetes often changes the recommendation.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:43:    "BMI 35 to 39: most people in this range are eligible for a surgical assessment, with or without other health conditions.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:44:    "BMI 40 and over: almost everyone in this range is eligible for surgery, and it is frequently the most effective long-term option.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:69:        "Once everything is in place, your procedure is scheduled. Our Perth-based aftercare team supports you through recovery and the months that follow, because the long-term result depends on the support around the surgery, not just the operation itself.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:116:      question: "My BMI is around 30. Am I too low for weight loss surgery?",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:118:        "Not necessarily. A BMI of 30 to 34 can qualify for surgery when you also have a weight-related health condition such as type 2 diabetes, sleep apnoea or high blood pressure. If you do not have those conditions, surgery may not be the first step, and our medical team can often help with weight loss medication and dietitian support instead. The only way to know for sure is an assessment, where your surgeon looks at the full picture rather than the number alone.",
src/app/osw/content/am-i-eligible/page.tsx:148:        "Then you will leave knowing that, with a clear explanation of why and what your alternatives are. Eligibility is not all-or-nothing. Our medical team supports patients with weight loss medication and dietitian-led programs, which can be the right path for people at a lower BMI or those who prefer to start without surgery. The goal of the assessment is the right answer for you, not a surgery booking.",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:7:  metaDescription: "Gastric sleeve surgery in Perth at Obesity Surgery WA, led by Dr Harsha Chandraratna, FRACS. Learn about eligibility, costs, and what to expect.",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:9:  heroSubheadline: "The gastric sleeve is the most commonly performed bariatric procedure in Australia. Obesity Surgery WA has been performing laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy in Perth for over a decade, with a dedicated team covering surgery, medical management, and long-term nutritional support.",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:18:    "A sleeve gastrectomy removes approximately 75-80% of the stomach, reshaping what remains into a narrow, sleeve-shaped tube. The new stomach holds considerably less food and produces less ghrelin, the hormone that drives hunger, which is why patients typically feel full sooner and experience reduced appetite after recovery.",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:20:    "The procedure is performed laparoscopically through small incisions using a camera, under general anaesthetic. Most patients spend around two nights in hospital before returning home to a liquid diet for the first few weeks, then progressing through pureed, soft, and normal foods over several months.",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:22:  comparisonNote: "Comparing sleeve to bypass or band? Each procedure has different weight loss patterns, recovery times, and risk profiles. The OSWA team can discuss which option makes the most sense for your specific health history and goals at your orientation consultation.",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:23:  eligibilityBmi: "Generally suitable for patients with a BMI of 35 or above, or BMI 30-35 with weight-related health conditions such as Type 2 diabetes, hypertension, or sleep apnoea.",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:25:    "BMI 35+, typically eligible for surgical assessment",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:26:    "BMI 30-35 with comorbidities such as Type 2 diabetes, hypertension, sleep apnoea, or severe joint problems",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:27:    "BMI 27-30, may be suitable for a medical weight management consultation at OSWA",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:36:    { title: "Hospital Recovery", description: "Most patients spend around two nights in hospital. Pain is managed with medication and most patients are mobile within hours of surgery. You will begin sips of clear fluids before leaving hospital." },
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:51:    "Staple line leak, rare (around 1-3%) but the most serious short-term complication; managed in hospital",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:57:  costParagraph: "The total cost of gastric sleeve surgery in Perth varies depending on surgeon fees, anaesthetist fees, hospital fees, and the extent of your private health insurance cover. Patients with private health insurance covering obesity surgery (item numbers 31575 (sleeve) and 31572 (bypass)) may have a significant portion of costs covered, subject to waiting periods and fund-specific limits. Medicare provides rebates on surgical and medical consultation fees. Some patients also access their superannuation on compassionate grounds to cover out-of-pocket costs. The OSWA team will provide you with a detailed cost estimate before you commit to any procedure.",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:62:  outcomesNote: "Patients treated by the Obesity Surgery WA team have achieved sustained weight loss across a range of bariatric procedures. Weight loss patterns vary. Factors including starting BMI, adherence to post-operative dietary guidance, activity levels, and individual metabolic responses all influence outcomes. The OSWA dietitian team works closely with patients in the first year to maximise long-term success and catch nutritional issues early. No specific outcome can be guaranteed. The team will give you realistic expectations based on your individual assessment.",
src/app/osw/content/preview/page.tsx:65:    { question: "Will I be able to eat normally after a gastric sleeve?", answer: "You will eat smaller portions permanently, but the types of food you can eat are largely unrestricted after full recovery. In the first few months you progress from liquids to pureed foods to soft foods to normal textures. Most patients settle into a pattern of three small meals per day, sometimes with healthy snacks." },
src/app/osw/content/non-surgical-weight-loss/page.tsx:5:  title: "Non-Surgical Weight Loss Perth | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/non-surgical-weight-loss/page.tsx:7:    "Non-surgical weight loss options in Perth at Obesity Surgery WA. GLP-1 medications, medical management and specialist nutrition support from a dedicated team.",
src/app/osw/content/non-surgical-weight-loss/page.tsx:17:    "Non-surgical weight loss options in Perth at Obesity Surgery WA. GLP-1 medications, medical management and specialist nutrition support from a dedicated team.",
src/app/osw/content/non-surgical-weight-loss/page.tsx:20:    "Not everyone is ready for surgery, and not everyone needs it. If your BMI sits in the 27 to 35 range, or you simply want to try a less invasive path first, we offer a genuine medical weight loss pathway: GLP-1 medications, supervised programs and dietitian support. This is a real option, not a consolation prize.",
src/app/osw/content/non-surgical-weight-loss/page.tsx:23:      label: "Suits BMI from",
src/app/osw/content/non-surgical-weight-loss/page.tsx:45:    "Non-surgical weight loss covers the options that help you lose weight without an operation. At Obesity Surgery WA this means medical weight management led by our physicians, prescription medications where they are appropriate, structured very-low-calorie programs, and ongoing support from our dietitian team. It is a proper, supervised pathway rather than a list of generic tips.",
src/app/osw/content/non-surgical-weight-loss/page.tsx:53:    "Generally suited to adults with a BMI of 30 or above, or a BMI of 27 or above when there are weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure or sleep apnoea. Your individual suitability is always assessed at consultation.",
src/app/osw/content/non-surgical-weight-loss/page.tsx:55:    "Your BMI sits roughly in the 27 to 35 range, where non-surgical options are often the sensible first step.",
src/app/osw/content/non-surgical-weight-loss/page.tsx:95:    "A genuine, supervised pathway you can start sooner, with no operation, no hospital stay and no surgical recovery.",
src/app/osw/content/non-surgical-weight-loss/page.tsx:134:        "It is a genuine pathway in its own right. For many people, particularly those with a BMI in the 27 to 35 range, medication, a supervised VLCD program or ESG can deliver meaningful results without an operation. We only suggest surgery when it is the better fit for your situation, and plenty of patients do well without ever needing it.",
src/app/osw/content/non-surgical-weight-loss/page.tsx:144:        "These medications are generally considered for adults with a BMI of 30 or above, or a BMI of 27 or above when there are weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure or sleep apnoea. A physician assessment confirms whether a medication is safe and appropriate for you specifically, taking your full health picture into account.",
src/app/osw/content/book-consultation/page.tsx:5:  title: "Book a Bariatric Consultation Perth | Obesity Surgery WA",
src/app/osw/content/book-consultation/page.tsx:6:  description: "Book your bariatric consultation at Obesity Surgery WA Perth. Phone or online. Our team will help you understand your options with no pressure to decide.",
src/app/osw/content/book-consultation/page.tsx:16:    "Book your bariatric consultation at Obesity Surgery WA Perth. Phone or online. Our team will help you understand your options with no pressure to decide.",
src/app/osw/content/book-consultation/page.tsx:32:    "You will have time to ask anything you like. We will explain the different procedures, walk through what recovery and aftercare look like, and answer questions about costs and funding openly, so there are no surprises later. You will also meet members of the team who would support you, including the clinical staff who handle the day-to-day care, before you make any decision at all.",
src/app/osw/content/book-consultation/page.tsx:43:    "You have questions about cost, recovery, time off work, or what life looks like afterwards.",
src/app/osw/content/book-consultation/page.tsx:131:        "Your consultation runs around 30 to 60 minutes. We talk through your health history and what you are hoping for, explain the different procedures and what recovery looks like, discuss costs openly, and answer your questions. You also meet members of the team who would care for you. There is no obligation and no pressure to decide anything on the day.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:5:  title: "GP Referral Information | Obesity Surgery WA",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:7:    "How to get a GP referral for bariatric surgery in Perth, why it unlocks Medicare rebates, and how GPs can refer a patient to Obesity Surgery WA.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:17:    "How to get a GP referral for bariatric surgery in Perth, why it unlocks Medicare rebates, and how GPs can refer a patient to Obesity Surgery WA.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:29:    "A GP referral is a letter from your general practitioner asking a specialist, in this case a bariatric surgeon at Obesity Surgery WA, to assess and treat you. It is the standard way the Australian health system connects you with specialist care, and for weight loss surgery it is almost always the first step.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:34:    "A useful referral should include your current weight and height (or BMI), relevant medical history, any weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, sleep apnoea or high blood pressure, current medications, and previous weight loss attempts. A standard GP referral to a specialist is valid for 12 months from the date it is written. You can self-refer and be seen, but only a GP referral gives you access to Medicare rebates.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:36:    "Bariatric surgery is generally considered for patients with a BMI of 35+ or BMI 30+ with related health conditions. These are starting points only. Your surgeon confirms what is appropriate at your consultation.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:38:    "For patients: ask your GP for a referral to Obesity Surgery WA. Your GP can address it to the practice and we will arrange your consultation.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:40:    "For GPs: a referral should note current BMI, relevant medical and weight history, comorbidities, current medications, and previous weight loss attempts.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:48:        "Ask your GP for a referral to Obesity Surgery WA. This gives you access to Medicare rebates and lets us review your history before we meet.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:68:        "You recover in hospital under the care of our team, usually for a few nights, with nursing and dietitian support before you head home.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:94:    "Costs depend on the procedure you and your surgeon decide on, your level of private health cover, and your individual circumstances. A valid GP referral is what lets you claim Medicare rebates, and most bariatric procedures attract a Medicare item number. Patients with appropriate private hospital cover often pay an out-of-pocket gap rather than the full fee, and some people choose to access their superannuation early to fund surgery. We give you a clear written estimate before you commit to anything, so there are no surprises.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:100:        "Lead surgeon and founder of Obesity Surgery WA. Fellow of the Royal Australasian College of Surgeons and lecturer at the University of Notre Dame. Operates at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco, with personal experience of bariatric surgery that shapes his patient-centred approach.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:120:        "Book an appointment with your GP and ask for a referral to Obesity Surgery WA. Your GP will discuss your weight and health history, and write a referral addressed to the practice. If you are not sure how to start the conversation, you can call our team on (08) 9332 0066 and we will explain what to ask for.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:135:        "A useful referral includes your current weight and height or BMI, relevant medical history, any weight-related conditions such as type 2 diabetes, sleep apnoea or high blood pressure, your current medications, and any previous weight loss attempts. This helps your surgeon prepare before you meet, but a brief referral is enough to get started.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:138:      question: "I am a GP. How do I refer a patient to Obesity Surgery WA?",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:140:        "Write a referral addressed to Obesity Surgery WA and have your patient contact us to book, or phone our team on (08) 9332 0066 to arrange the referral and discuss the best way to send it through. We will confirm the details with you and your patient and arrange a consultation at our Booragoon or Mandurah rooms.",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:148:      question: "Where will my consultation be, and which hospitals are used?",
src/app/osw/content/gp-referral/page.tsx:150:        "Consultations are held at our Booragoon rooms at Garden City Specialist Centre (537 Marmion Street) and in Mandurah at WA Cardiology Centre (30 Minilya Pkwy). Surgery is performed at St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco, two accredited private hospitals in Perth.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:5:  title: "Weight Loss Surgery FAQs Perth | Obesity Surgery WA",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:7:    "Answers to the most common questions about weight loss surgery in Perth: eligibility, procedures, cost, Medicare, safety, recovery and life after surgery.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:17:    "Answers to the most common questions about weight loss surgery in Perth: eligibility, procedures, cost, Medicare, safety, recovery and life after surgery.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:20:    "Answers to the most common questions about weight loss surgery in Perth, gathered in one place. If you are still working things out, this is a good place to start. We cover eligibility, the different procedures, cost and Medicare, safety and recovery, and what life is really like after surgery, in plain language and with no pressure to decide anything today.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:29:    "Deciding whether weight loss surgery is right for you raises a lot of questions, and most people arrive with the same ones. This page brings together the questions we hear most often at Obesity Surgery WA, so you can get straight answers before you ever pick up the phone. It is meant as an orientation, not the final word, because the answer that actually matters is the one your surgeon gives you about your own situation.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:30:    "Weight loss surgery, also called bariatric surgery, covers a range of procedures that change the size or function of the stomach to help you eat less and feel full sooner. At Obesity Surgery WA we offer gastric sleeve, gastric bypass, mini gastric bypass, gastric band and revision surgery, as well as weight-loss medication and other non-surgical options for people who are not ready for, or not suited to, an operation. Surgery is one tool among several, not the only road.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:36:    "As a general guide, people with a BMI of 35 or above are typically suitable for a surgical assessment, and a BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify when weight-related health conditions are present. These are starting points, not a final ruling. Your surgeon confirms what is right for you.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:38:    "BMI is part of the picture, not the whole of it. Eligibility is a whole-person assessment that also considers your health conditions, your history of previous attempts, and your goals.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:48:        "Ask your GP for a referral to Obesity Surgery WA. This gives you access to Medicare rebates and lets us review your history before we meet. You can also contact us directly if you would rather start that way.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:68:        "You recover in hospital under the care of our team, usually for a few nights, with nursing and dietitian support before you head home.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:76:  benefitsTitle: "Why patients choose Obesity Surgery WA",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:80:    "Operating privileges at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco, accredited private hospitals with full theatre and recovery facilities.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:86:    "No surgeon, however experienced, can promise a particular result, and you should be cautious of anyone who does. Bariatric surgery is a powerful tool, but it works alongside your effort, not instead of it. Our role is to give you a carefully planned operation in an accredited hospital and the support to make the most of it, while being straight with you about the risks any surgery carries. The honest answer to many of the questions below is that it depends on you, your starting health and the procedure you and your surgeon choose.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:95:    "Cost depends on the procedure you and your surgeon decide on, your level of private health cover, and your individual circumstances. Most bariatric procedures attract a Medicare item number, and patients with appropriate private hospital cover often pay an out-of-pocket gap rather than the full fee. Medicare contributes toward the surgical, anaesthetist and specialist consultation fees, but does not cover the hospital stay or theatre, which is where private hospital cover comes in. Some people apply to access their superannuation early on medical grounds, and third-party payment plans are sometimes used. We give you a clear, itemised written estimate before you commit to anything, so there are no surprises.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:101:        "Lead surgeon and founder of Obesity Surgery WA. Fellow of the Royal Australasian College of Surgeons and lecturer at the University of Notre Dame. Operates at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco, with personal experience of bariatric surgery that shapes his patient-centred approach.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:116:        "As a general guide, people with a BMI of 35 or above are typically suitable for a surgical assessment, and those at 40 or above are almost always suitable. A BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify when weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes are present. BMI is only part of the picture though. Eligibility is a whole-person assessment that also considers your health conditions, your history of previous attempts and your goals, and your surgeon confirms what is right for you at consultation.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:119:      question: "My BMI is around 30. Am I too low for weight loss surgery?",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:121:        "Not necessarily. A BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify for surgery when a weight-related health condition is present, and type 2 diabetes in particular can change the recommendation. If surgery is not the right starting point for you, our medical team can often help with weight-loss medication and dietitian support instead. Wherever you start, our team can talk you through the options that fit your situation.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:126:        "At Obesity Surgery WA we offer gastric sleeve (sleeve gastrectomy), gastric bypass, mini gastric bypass, adjustable gastric band and revision surgery for patients who have had previous bariatric surgery. We also offer weight-loss medication and other non-surgical options for people who are not ready for, or not suited to, an operation. Your surgeon helps you understand which of these fits your body, your health history and your goals.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:136:        "Modern bariatric surgery is well established and is performed using keyhole (laparoscopic) techniques at accredited private hospitals, in our case St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco, with full theatre and recovery facilities. That said, all surgery carries risk, including bleeding, infection, blood clots, leaks and reactions to anaesthetic. We will not minimise this. Your surgeon goes through your individual risk profile in detail at your assessment so you can make a clear, informed decision.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:141:        "There is no single price because the total is made up of separate fees billed by different providers, including the surgeon, the anaesthetist and the hospital. The amount you pay depends on the procedure, your private health cover and your individual circumstances. Most procedures attract a Medicare item number, and patients with appropriate private hospital cover often pay an out-of-pocket gap rather than the full fee. We provide a clear, itemised written estimate before you commit to anything.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:146:        "When you meet the clinical criteria and have a GP referral, Medicare contributes a rebate toward eligible surgical, anaesthetist and specialist consultation fees. Medicare does not cover the full cost, and it does not cover the hospital stay or theatre fees, which is the role of private hospital cover. A private contribution, or gap, typically remains. Our team can explain what applies in your situation before you commit.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:151:        "Private hospital cover that specifically includes bariatric surgery is the usual way to fund the hospital side, and many funds apply a 12-month waiting period and require a higher tier policy, so it is worth confirming the exact item numbers with your fund. If you do not have suitable cover, some patients apply to access their superannuation early on medical grounds, which is assessed by the ATO and your super fund rather than by us. Third-party payment plans are also sometimes used. We can talk you through these options.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:164:      question: "How long is the recovery, and how soon can I go back to work?",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:166:        "Most patients stay in hospital for a few nights after surgery, with nursing and dietitian support before heading home. Time off work is generally around two to four weeks, depending on the procedure and how physical your job is. Office-based work is often possible sooner, while heavy lifting needs longer. Your surgeon gives you advice tailored to your situation and your recovery.",
src/app/osw/content/faqs/page.tsx:181:        "We consult at our Booragoon rooms in the Garden City Specialist Centre (537 Marmion Street) and in Mandurah at the WA Cardiology Centre (30 Minilya Pkwy). Surgery is performed at St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco, two accredited private hospitals in Perth.",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:5:  title: "Hiatus Hernia Repair | Obesity Surgery WA Perth",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:7:    "Hiatus hernia repair explained: symptoms, when surgery is needed, the laparoscopic approach, recovery and cost. Upper GI specialists at Obesity Surgery WA, Perth.",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:17:    "Hiatus hernia repair explained: symptoms, when surgery is needed, the laparoscopic approach, recovery and cost. Upper GI specialists at Obesity Surgery WA, Perth.",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:24:    { label: "Hospital stay", value: "1 to 2 nights", note: "Most patients go home within a day or two; recovery varies" },
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:25:    { label: "Where", value: "SJOG Murdoch & Subiaco", note: "Performed at St John of God private hospitals in Perth" },
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:31:    "The operation is almost always done laparoscopically, through several small keyhole incisions rather than one large cut. This generally means less pain, a shorter hospital stay and a quicker return to normal activity than open surgery. Larger or more complex hernias can be more demanding, and your surgeon will explain what your particular hernia involves before any decision is made.",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:36:    "There is no BMI threshold for a hiatus hernia repair. This is a medically indicated upper GI operation, so the decision rests on your symptoms, your investigations and how well other treatments have worked, rather than on a number.",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:87:    "Hiatus hernia repair is a well-established and generally safe operation, but like any surgery it carries risks, and you deserve a clear picture before you decide. The fundoplication wrap can, in some people, cause temporary difficulty swallowing or a feeling of bloating and an inability to belch, which usually eases over the first weeks to months. As with any procedure there are general surgical risks, and occasionally a hernia can recur over time. None of this is said to alarm you, and most patients do well. Your surgeon will talk through exactly how these risks apply to your situation.",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:91:    "General surgical risks such as bleeding, infection, blood clots or a reaction to the anaesthetic.",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:96:    "Cost is a fair thing to ask about early. The final figure depends on your private health cover, your surgeon and anaesthetist fees, the length of your hospital stay and whether any additional procedures are needed at the same time. Because a hiatus hernia repair is a medically indicated operation, Medicare and most private health funds with appropriate hospital cover contribute towards eligible cases. Rather than quote a single number that may not apply to you, we give every patient a clear, itemised written estimate before any decision is made, so you know where you stand.",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:130:      question: "What is recovery like after a hiatus hernia repair?",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:132:        "Most patients stay in hospital for one to two nights. Because of the wrap, you will usually follow a liquid then soft diet for a few weeks while everything settles, gradually returning to normal food as your surgeon advises. Some difficulty swallowing or bloating in the early weeks is common and tends to ease. Many people return to light activity within a couple of weeks, though recovery varies and you should follow your surgeon's specific guidance.",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:142:        "There is no single figure, because the total depends on your private health cover, the surgeon and anaesthetist fees, your length of stay and whether any other procedure is done at the same time. As a medically indicated operation, eligible cases attract a Medicare rebate and a contribution from most private health funds with appropriate hospital cover. We provide a clear, itemised written estimate before any decision, so you understand your likely out-of-pocket cost in advance.",
src/app/osw/content/hiatus-hernia-repair/page.tsx:147:        "Hiatus hernia repairs are performed at St John of God Murdoch or St John of God Subiaco, both well-equipped private hospitals in Perth. You will need a referral from your GP, which allows you to claim Medicare rebates on your consultations and is the starting point for seeing one of our surgeons. If you are unsure how to arrange the referral, our team can talk you through it when you call.",

