--- fix-spec-2.md ---
# Gate-2 fix spec — per-page fix worker (round 2)

Same ownership rules as before: ONE page, its 3 files only (docs review, content-reviews/<slug>.md live copy, content-briefs/<slug>.md). Keep live↔docs parity (live = no line refs/paths). Smallest sufficient change. Q1/Q2 stay OPEN.

Root problem this round: OUR OWN replacement text introduced new claims. Principles:
1. Canonical BMI wording everywhere: "BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment." No "BMI 35+ with" shorthand.
2. No quantified outcome claims in paste-ready copy (60–70%, 70–80%, operation times, stay lengths, recovery timelines, "substantial long-term weight loss", "most patients … around two nights"). Replace with qualitative, individually-assessed wording; where a stat is strategically wanted, keep it OUT of the paste-ready copy and add a "verify: requires a cited clinical source + practice sign-off before publication" note instead.
3. No comparative clinical claims ("less malabsorptive than a bypass", "reduce surgical risk"). State mechanism neutrally (e.g. "does not reroute the small intestine; nutritional monitoring is still required"; pre-op diet "may be prescribed to reduce liver size — purpose and risks explained individually").
4. Unverifiable assertions get placeholders: "Medically reviewed by…" → "[clinical reviewer to be verified]" or omit; hospital names in NEW rewrite copy → "[hospital identity to be verified]". Do NOT delete the briefs' existing SJOG mentions (that would resolve Q1) — annotate them "(hospital identity to be verified — open question)" if not already.
5. Canonical 30/30 FAQ answer (use EXACTLY this on every page that has the 30/30 FAQ, so pages don't conflict): "The 30/30 rule is an eating habit often recommended after bariatric surgery: take around 30 minutes to eat each meal, and avoid drinking fluids for 30 minutes either side of eating. Your dietitian will confirm the exact protocol the practice recommends for you. [30/30 protocol to be confirmed with the practice before publication]"
6. No invented medical lists: don't present PCOS/fatty liver/reflux etc. as qualifying "major" MBS comorbidities (say conditions are assessed individually against MBS criteria); replace the new contraindication list (pregnancy/substance misuse/eating disorders) with general clinical-risk/aftercare-assessment wording.
7. GLP-1 must be labelled "OPEN — pending TGA/legal decision" — the review must not say GLP-1 references are prohibited, nor allow them; class/ingredient-only interim rule stands.
8. Cost wording: never claim "no single published price" (contradicts the source); say costs vary and current estimates are provided at consultation.
9. Internal-link anchors must not promise features (no "calculate your BMI" if no calculator; use "understand how BMI is assessed").

Print a short per-finding report when done.
