@@ -20,7 +20,7 @@
 
 > A gastric bypass is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30-35 with associated weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux.
 
-> Gastric bypass suitability is assessed individually. For Medicare-funded bariatric surgery, MBS criteria generally describe clinically severe obesity as a BMI of 40 or higher, or a BMI of 35 or higher with a major medical comorbidity. A BMI below those thresholds does not meet the MBS eligibility criteria; your surgeon will consider your full health picture and confirm whether any other clinical or rebate rule applies.
+> Gastric bypass suitability is assessed individually. BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment.
 
 Why it matters: MBS explanatory note TN.8.29 generally describes clinically severe obesity as BMI 40 or higher, or BMI 35 or higher with major medical comorbidities; the writer followed the brief as instructed, so the brief also needs correction.
 
@@ -60,7 +60,9 @@
 
 > Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 70-80% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
 
-> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary and no outcome is guaranteed.
+> Weight-loss outcomes vary between individuals and depend on starting health, nutrition, activity and follow-up; your surgeon can discuss realistic expectations without promising a particular result.
+>
+> Verification note (not paste-ready): retain the 70–80% figure only if a cited clinical source and practice sign-off are obtained before publication.
 
 > Often relieves acid reflux and heartburn, unlike some procedures that can worsen it.
 
@@ -80,7 +82,9 @@
 
 > On average, gastric bypass patients lose around 70–80% of their excess body weight over 12–18 months (slightly more than the sleeve on average). Individual results vary based on starting weight, age, diet, and activity levels, and your surgeon will give you a realistic expectation based on your individual circumstances.
 
-> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess body weight over 12–18 months, but individual results vary by starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.
+> Weight-loss outcomes vary between individuals and depend on starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.
+>
+> Verification note (not paste-ready): retain the 70–80% figure only if a cited clinical source and practice sign-off are obtained before publication.
 
 > Most people adapt well though and many find their relationship with food improves meaningfully over time.
 
@@ -243,7 +247,7 @@
 
 > Missing - no draft sentence or section provides the required stats bar.
 
-> Expected weight loss: 70-80% of excess weight, clinical average; results vary. Time in surgery: about 120 minutes under general anaesthetic using a keyhole technique. Hospital stay: usually 2-3 nights. Return to most activity: about 3-4 weeks, with light duties often sooner and heavy lifting later.
+> Retain this four-stat bar as brief-only strategy, not paste-ready copy: expected weight loss 70–80% of excess weight, clinical average; time in surgery about 120 minutes under general anaesthetic using a keyhole technique; hospital stay usually 2–3 nights; return to most activity about 3–4 weeks, with light duties often sooner and heavy lifting later. **Verify: requires a cited clinical source + practice sign-off before publication.**
 
 Why it matters: The brief requires this quick factual orientation and it helps readers scan the procedure at a glance.
 
@@ -251,7 +255,7 @@
 
 > After your bypass you'll spend 2-3 nights recuperating at [<u>Mount Private Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [<u>Waikiki Private Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth.
 
-> After your bypass, you will usually spend 2-3 nights recovering in the hospital location confirmed by your surgeon, depending on theatre availability and clinical needs.
+> After your bypass, your hospital stay will take place at [hospital identity to be verified]. Length of stay and recovery timing depend on clinical needs and will be confirmed by your surgeon.
 
 Why it matters: The hospital names conflict with the brief, but the draft may reflect newer client information, so verify the correct hospital name(s) with the client before changing either direction.
 

