-> After your bypass, you will usually spend 2-3 nights recovering in the hospital location confirmed by your surgeon, depending on theatre availability and clinical needs.
+> After your bypass, your hospital stay will take place at [hospital identity to be verified]. Length of stay and recovery timing depend on clinical needs and will be confirmed by your surgeon.
 
 Why it matters: The hospital names conflict with the brief, but the draft may reflect newer client information, so verify the correct hospital name(s) with the client before changing either direction.
 
@@ -311,7 +315,7 @@
 
 > Missing - no draft sentence answers the 30/30 rule for gastric bypass.
 
-> **What is the 30/30 rule after gastric bypass?** The 30/30 rule usually means avoiding fluids for about 30 minutes before and after meals so you can focus on small portions and slow eating. Your dietitian will confirm whether that timing is right for you after surgery.
+> **What is the 30/30 rule after gastric bypass?** The 30/30 rule is an eating habit often recommended after bariatric surgery: take around 30 minutes to eat each meal, and avoid drinking fluids for 30 minutes either side of eating. Your dietitian will confirm the exact protocol the practice recommends for you. [30/30 protocol to be confirmed with the practice before publication]
 
 Why it matters: This question appears in current Google People-Also-Ask results for bypass and bariatric searches.
 
@@ -327,7 +331,7 @@
 
 > Missing - no FAQ directly answers "How much does gastric bypass cost in Perth?"
 
-> **How much does gastric bypass cost in Perth?** There is no single published price. Any indicative range and an itemised estimate are discussed at consultation after the team considers surgeon, anaesthetist and hospital costs, Medicare and private health cover; the written estimate should show likely out-of-pocket costs before you decide.
+> **How much does gastric bypass cost in Perth?** Costs vary by surgeon, anaesthetist, hospital and private health cover. Current estimates and an itemised breakdown are provided at consultation before you decide, with Medicare and private-health contributions subject to eligibility, policy terms and applicable rules.
 
 Why it matters: The current heading is national even though search demand is local.
 
@@ -359,7 +363,7 @@
 
 > Calculate your BMI.
 
-> Use our [am I eligible for weight loss surgery guide](/am-i-eligible) to calculate your BMI and understand the other factors your surgeon will consider.
+> Use our [am I eligible for weight loss surgery guide](/am-i-eligible) to understand how BMI is assessed and what other factors your surgeon will consider.
 
 Why it matters: This turns a dead instruction into a useful internal pathway.
 
@@ -391,7 +395,7 @@
 
 > FAQs (8 questions)
 
-> **Who is not eligible for gastric bypass?** For Medicare-funded bariatric surgery, a BMI below 40 without a major medical comorbidity, or a BMI below 35, does not meet the stated MBS eligibility threshold. Surgery may also be unsuitable if health risks, anatomy or the ability to follow long-term nutrition and follow-up need further assessment. Eligibility and alternatives must be discussed with your surgeon.
+> **Who is not eligible for gastric bypass?** For Medicare-funded bariatric surgery, the usual wording is BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. A BMI below these thresholds does not meet the stated MBS criteria; surgery may also be unsuitable if health risks, anatomy or the ability to follow long-term nutrition and follow-up need further assessment.
 
 Why it matters: The page should answer the negative eligibility question without implying that a condition, BMI or online checklist guarantees access to surgery.
 

