@@ -20,7 +20,7 @@
 
 > A gastric bypass is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30-35 with associated weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux.
 
-> Gastric bypass suitability is assessed individually. For Medicare-funded bariatric surgery, MBS criteria generally describe clinically severe obesity as a BMI of 40 or higher, or a BMI of 35 or higher with a major medical comorbidity. A BMI below those thresholds does not meet the MBS eligibility criteria; your surgeon will consider your full health picture and confirm whether any other clinical or rebate rule applies.
+> Gastric bypass suitability is assessed individually. BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment.
 
 Why it matters: MBS explanatory note TN.8.29 generally describes clinically severe obesity as BMI 40 or higher, or BMI 35 or higher with major medical comorbidities; the writer followed the brief as instructed, so the brief also needs correction.
 
@@ -68,7 +68,9 @@
 
 > Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 70-80% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
 
-> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary and no outcome is guaranteed.
+> Weight-loss outcomes vary between individuals and depend on starting health, nutrition, activity and follow-up; your surgeon can discuss realistic expectations without promising a particular result.
+>
+> Verification note (not paste-ready): retain the 70–80% figure only if a cited clinical source and practice sign-off are obtained before publication.
 
 > Often relieves acid reflux and heartburn, unlike some procedures that can worsen it.
 
@@ -94,7 +96,9 @@
 
 > On average, gastric bypass patients lose around 70–80% of their excess body weight over 12–18 months (slightly more than the sleeve on average). Individual results vary based on starting weight, age, diet, and activity levels, and your surgeon will give you a realistic expectation based on your individual circumstances.
 
-> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess body weight over 12–18 months, but individual results vary by starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.
+> Weight-loss outcomes vary between individuals and depend on starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.
+>
+> Verification note (not paste-ready): retain the 70–80% figure only if a cited clinical source and practice sign-off are obtained before publication.
 
 Source: extracted draft L291-L297.
 
@@ -253,7 +257,7 @@
 >
 > The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at three locations: [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/). Pre- and post-operative (once you’ve been discharged from hospital) consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre, Booragoon, and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
 >
-> The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at the hospital location(s) confirmed by your surgeon. Pre- and post-operative consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre in Booragoon and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
+> The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at [hospital identity to be verified]. Pre- and post-operative consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre in Booragoon and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
 
 > Keep the Medicare/private-health detail on `/medicare-private-health-cover`.
 
@@ -271,7 +275,7 @@
 
 > Missing - no draft sentence or section provides the required stats bar.
 
-> Expected weight loss: 70-80% of excess weight, clinical average; results vary. Time in surgery: about 120 minutes under general anaesthetic using a keyhole technique. Hospital stay: usually 2-3 nights. Return to most activity: about 3-4 weeks, with light duties often sooner and heavy lifting later.
+> Retain this four-stat bar as brief-only strategy, not paste-ready copy: expected weight loss 70–80% of excess weight, clinical average; time in surgery about 120 minutes under general anaesthetic using a keyhole technique; hospital stay usually 2–3 nights; return to most activity about 3–4 weeks, with light duties often sooner and heavy lifting later. **Verify: requires a cited clinical source + practice sign-off before publication.**
 
 Why it matters: The brief requires this quick factual orientation and it helps readers scan the procedure at a glance.
 
@@ -279,7 +283,7 @@
 
 > After your bypass you'll spend 2-3 nights recuperating at [<u>Mount Private Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), or [<u>Waikiki Private Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/) in Perth.
 
-> After your bypass, you will usually spend 2-3 nights recovering in the hospital location confirmed by your surgeon, depending on theatre availability and clinical needs.
+> After your bypass, your hospital stay will take place at [hospital identity to be verified]. Length of stay and recovery timing depend on clinical needs and will be confirmed by your surgeon.
 
 Why it matters: The hospital names conflict with the brief, but the draft may reflect newer client information, so verify the correct hospital name(s) with the client before changing either direction.
 
@@ -347,7 +351,7 @@
 
 > Missing - no draft sentence answers the 30/30 rule for gastric bypass.
 
-> **What is the 30/30 rule after gastric bypass?** The 30/30 rule usually means avoiding fluids for about 30 minutes before and after meals so you can focus on small portions and slow eating. Your dietitian will confirm whether that timing is right for you after surgery.
+> **What is the 30/30 rule after gastric bypass?** The 30/30 rule is an eating habit often recommended after bariatric surgery: take around 30 minutes to eat each meal, and avoid drinking fluids for 30 minutes either side of eating. Your dietitian will confirm the exact protocol the practice recommends for you. [30/30 protocol to be confirmed with the practice before publication]
 
 Why it matters: This question appears in current Google People-Also-Ask results for bypass and bariatric searches.
 
@@ -367,7 +371,7 @@
 
 > Missing - no FAQ directly answers "How much does gastric bypass cost in Perth?"
 
-> **How much does gastric bypass cost in Perth?** There is no single published price. Any indicative range and an itemised estimate are discussed at consultation after the team considers surgeon, anaesthetist and hospital costs, Medicare and private health cover; the written estimate should show likely out-of-pocket costs before you decide.
+> **How much does gastric bypass cost in Perth?** Costs vary by surgeon, anaesthetist, hospital and private health cover. Current estimates and an itemised breakdown are provided at consultation before you decide, with Medicare and private-health contributions subject to eligibility, policy terms and applicable rules.
 
 Why it matters: The current heading is national even though search demand is local.
 
@@ -403,7 +407,7 @@
 
 > Calculate your BMI.
 
-> Use our [am I eligible for weight loss surgery guide](/am-i-eligible) to calculate your BMI and understand the other factors your surgeon will consider.
+> Use our [am I eligible for weight loss surgery guide](/am-i-eligible) to understand how BMI is assessed and what other factors your surgeon will consider.
 
 Why it matters: This turns a dead instruction into a useful internal pathway.
 
@@ -431,7 +435,7 @@
 
 > The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at three locations: [<u>Mount Private Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [<u>Waikiki Private Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/). Pre- and post-operative (once you’ve been discharged from hospital) consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre, Booragoon, and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
 
-> The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at the hospital location(s) confirmed by your surgeon. Pre- and post-operative consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre in Booragoon and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
+> The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at [hospital identity to be verified]. Pre- and post-operative consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre in Booragoon and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
 
 Why it matters: Q1 remains open. Verify the hospital identity with the client before replacing the draft list; the neutral replacement keeps the page publishable without choosing between conflicting sources.
 
@@ -441,7 +445,7 @@
 
 > FAQs (8 questions)
 
-> **Who is not eligible for gastric bypass?** For Medicare-funded bariatric surgery, a BMI below 40 without a major medical comorbidity, or a BMI below 35, does not meet the stated MBS eligibility threshold. Surgery may also be unsuitable if health risks, anatomy or the ability to follow long-term nutrition and follow-up need further assessment. Eligibility and alternatives must be discussed with your surgeon.
+> **Who is not eligible for gastric bypass?** For Medicare-funded bariatric surgery, the usual wording is BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. A BMI below these thresholds does not meet the stated MBS criteria; surgery may also be unsuitable if health risks, anatomy or the ability to follow long-term nutrition and follow-up need further assessment.
 
 Why it matters: The page should answer the negative eligibility question without implying that a condition, BMI or online checklist guarantees access to surgery.
 
@@ -477,7 +481,7 @@
 
 > You may need to follow a specific pre-op diet to shrink your liver and reduce surgical risk (typically 2-4 weeks), but you won't do it alone!
 
-> You may need to follow a specific pre-operative diet for 2-4 weeks to reduce liver size and surgical risk, with guidance from the OSWA dietitian team.
+> A pre-operative diet may be prescribed to reduce liver size; its purpose, duration and risks will be explained individually, with guidance from the OSWA dietitian team.
 
 Why it matters: The rewrite keeps the support message while using a more clinical tone.
 

